\par\par \b \uc2  Commodities - Oil tumbles, gold and wheat advance. \b0\par\par\uc2 714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2000\par 23:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 25 (Reuters) - Oil prices closed sharply lower on Monday as speculators continued to exit the market after Friday's decision by the Clinton Administration to use strategic oil stockpiles to supply refineries. \par \par \uc2  In other commodity trading, gold prices edged higher in quiet trade as dealers nervously eyed the possibility of more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the weak euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Wheat closed higher on export prospects. \par \par \uc2  At the New York Mercantile Exchange, crude oil for November delivery closed $1.11 lower at $31.57 a barrel. That was down 16 percent from the post-Gulf War high of $37.80 that traded on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Energy Secretary Bill Richardson said on Friday the government would release 30 million barrels of oil over a 30-day period from the 571-million-barrel Strategic Petroleum Reserve (SPR), aiming to shore up supplies and stave off potential shortages of heating oil as winter approaches. \par \par \uc2  "It's a significant volume of oil," said International Energy Agency Executive Director Robert Priddle. "It's obviously more than the last increase in production announced by OPEC. It's obviously going to have a downward effect." \par \par \uc2  The SPR move followed calls for such action by Vice President Al Gore, who is battling Republican contender George W. Bush ahead of the presidential election on November 7. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, talk circulated that European Union members were considering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their oil markets, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  has done, but none announced such a move. \par \par \uc2  "The sense here, I think, was that this was a prudent step that others welcomed, and that others were looking into whether they could do similar things within their countries," U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers said in an interview in Prague where he is attending talks with world finance leaders. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, more jitters in the oil market over an Iraqi claim that its missile defences hit a Western war plane abated after the Pentagon denied that any American or British planes had been hit over a no-fly zone over northern Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Heating oil for October delivery closed 1.41 cents lower at 94.07 cents a gallon, while October gasoline fell 1.26 cents a gallon to 92.70 cents. \par \par \uc2  The SPR release, only the second since the Gulf War, is equivalent to 1 million barrels per day over a month, equal to 5 percent of the 20 million barrels in crude oil and refined products the U.S. consumes each day. \par \par \uc2  "We are prepared to take further action if necessary," Energy Secretary Bill Richardson said. \par \par \uc2  The SPR move came as oil prices continued to soar even after members of the Organisation of Petroleum Exporting Countries agreed to raise output by another 800,000 barrels per day beginning October 1. \par \par \uc2  At the COMEX, gold closed higher with many eyes on the euro's next move after Friday's concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  steadied the troubled European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Strength in the euro increases its buying power for dollar-denominated metals. \par \par \uc2  Gold for December delivery closed $2.20 higher at $277.30 an ounce. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets remained wary of more concerted support of the euro after the Group of Seven major economies pushed it sharply higher on Friday, prompting buying of gold to cover "short," or previously sold, positions from Thursday's 12-month price lows. \par \par \uc2  Traders said price dips to $272 and below were seeing some physical demand for gold from Asia. The wedding and festival season in India, the top consumer of gold, is getting under way, and gold demand usually strengthens during the September-October period, they said. \par \par \uc2  At the Chicago Board of Trade, corn and soybean prices edged lower on the burgeoning supply outlook despite some rain delays to harvest in the U.S. Midwest. Soybeans for November delivery closed 2-1/2 cents a bushel lower at $4.93-1/2, while December corn slipped 3/4 cent a bushel to $1.93-1/4. \par \par \uc2  Wheat bucked the trend, with December closing 2-3/4 cents higher at $2.53-1/2. Traders cited a strong export lineup for food aid shipments of U.S. wheat and fresh buying from Egypt, which they said bought 220,000 metric tons of U.S. wheat. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9p06se1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
