\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS WRAPUP 3-Techs lead bourse rise, oil tumbles. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Melanie Cheary \par \uc2 825 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2000\par 14:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 25 (Reuters) - A bounce-back in technology shares pulled stock markets pulled higher on Monday, after the world's top economies last week moved to prop up the euro and oil prices tumbled on a U.S. move to tap strategic oil reserves. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said investors remained cautious after chip maker Intel Corp.'s sales warning last week, and any sign of weakness in technology, media and telecommunications (TMT) shares would trigger another bout of profit-taking. \par \par \uc2  The stable euro cheered European bourses but \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers were sceptical, unconvinced the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had found a floor and remained alert for a repeat of last Friday's joint euro-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Group of Seven central banks. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' plan to release 30 million barrels of oil from its reserve, dragged oil prices lower and, while relief supported stock markets, worries persisted that oil could still return to recent highs and trigger an economic slowdown. \par \par \uc2  In Asia, Tokyo dealers doubted the G7's oil and euro moves were sufficient to turn the tide and the Nikkei average shed a large chunk of its early gains to finish 1.1 percent higher at 15,992.90 points. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people don't really want to invest at the moment, they're not sure about the outlook in America and they will be watching for further signs of euro \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a dealer. \par \par \uc2  A weekend statement by the G7 pointed to further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support for the euro and said G7 bankers would "cooperate in exchange markets as appropriate". \par \par \uc2  TMTs REBOUND, BUT WITH INTEL HANGOVER \par \par \uc2  Money moved out of energy stocks, bourse darlings during the six-week rally in oil prices, and into TMT's with key stocks like Cable & Wireless surging more than seven percent and Marconi Plc leaping almost 11 percent. \par \par \uc2  Royal Dutch Shell fell 2.7 percent and BP Amoco was 0.7 percent lower. \par \par \uc2  By 1148 GMT, the benchmark German DAX 30 index moved one percent higher to 6,809 points with Frankfurt's tech-rich Neuer Markt index .NMDKX50 rising 3.9 percent to 5,422 points. \par \par \uc2  London's FTSE 100 was 1.6 percent stronger at 6,303 points, with Vodafone Plc alone boosting the index 25 points with a four percent advance. \par \par \uc2  Still, strategists cautioned that the recovery could be short-lived with investors still jittery about the outlook for corporate earnings after Intel's revenue warning last week. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think this is going to last. The bounce in TMT is just catch-up after the late turnaround in the States on Friday and I think people will wait to see whether the euro has turned the corner," said David Thwaites, European strategist at BNP-Paribas. \par \par \uc2  STABLE EURO, EYES OUT FOR MORE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  By 1051 GMT, the euro was stable around $0.8765, underpinned above technical support at $0.8750/60 amid wariness of further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I think investors have to want to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and to buy it just because you think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is around the corner is never enough to provide a durable recovery," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  The single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed little reaction to stronger-than-expected German consumer price data which showed a preliminary September rise of 0.4 percent month-on-month and a 2.4 percent rise year-on-year. \par \par \uc2  OIL TUMBLES \par \par \uc2  Oil prices tipped lower after the United States decided to tap national reserves in a bid to avert a winter fuel crisis and analysts predicted that relief could ease prices down to $25 a barrel before year-end. \par \par \uc2  London Brent futures dipped briefly below $30 but then recovered to stand 80 cents lower at $30.41 a barrel by about 1150 GMT. That came on top of a $1.48 decline on Friday in anticipation of the release which will add a million barrels a day to U.S. supplies for a month. \par \par \uc2  U.S. light crude in electronic trade fell $1.20 to $31.48. \par \par \uc2  Energy Secretary Bill Richardson late on Friday announced the release of 30 million barrels of crude from the 571 million-barrel Strategic Petroleum Reserve - the first use of the SPR since Iraq's invasion of Kuwait in 1990. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the size of the release came as a surprise and the price decline could accelerate in coming weeks. \par \par \uc2  "No one expected it to be of this magnitude," said Lawrence Eagles of brokers GNI. "It could well serve to push prices down to more moderate levels." \par \par \uc2  "It will bring the price epicentre down. It will not bring a radically different situation but will fill in the gap in terms of market sentiment and remove panic," said David Brown, chief economist at Bear Stearns in London. He predicted $25 a barrel in the fourth quarter. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9p06r0v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
