\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro digs in, market ponders G7 resolve. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mariko Hayashibara \par \uc2 685 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2000\par 09:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 25 (Reuters) - The euro kept a tentative grip on 88 cents in edgy trade Monday as fears of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  warred with the market's ingrained cynicism towards the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  By late afternoon, the euro was hovering at $0.8800/05 and 94.76/90 yen after the Group of Seven's (G7) shock \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday lifted it from $0.8620 to as high as $0.9040 and 96.20 yen through brokers. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had fallen to a lifetime low of $0.8439 and 90.05 yen last week, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets were stunned by the concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the bloodied euro last Friday with the unexpected participation of the United States. The move was later backed up by industrial powers, in a statement over the weekend, pledging to monitor the markets closely and cooperate as appropriate. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Vice Finance Minister Toshiro Muto did his best to keep the market guessing on Monday, saying authorities are watching the euro carefully and will take action if needed. \par \par \uc2  All of which left dealers very wary of a repeat performance. \par \par \uc2  "It's obvious the G7 will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again - monetary authorities are particularly sensitive about their credibility so they're not likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  just once and go away," argued Ken Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Deutsche Bank. \par \par \uc2  DOUBT LINGERS \par \par \uc2  Many Japanese dealers were on high alert for further euro-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , possibly later in the day. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were especially wary of the Bank of Japan since it has historically been far more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  than its G7 counterparts and was believed to have been among the biggest buyers of euro on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "(Japanese) monetary authorities and Japanese investors are both willing to see the euro lifted to the 100 yen level before half-year business results (on Friday)," said a dealer at a Japanese trust bank. \par \par \uc2  "So I see a high possibility of them coming into the market later in the day at the earliest," he said. \par \par \uc2  But dealers were highly sceptical over whether \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would put the euro on a decisive recovery cycle. \par \par \uc2  "As the estimated amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't seem so large, this suggests that the concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not aiming at bringing the euro sharply higher. It was rather to stop the almost bottomless fall," said Shinichi Sato, manager at Tokyo-Mitsubishi Securities said. \par \par \uc2  Sato added that it would be difficult for the euro to stage a dramatic recovery as long as money keeps flowing from the euro zone to the United States, which was justified by the widening gap in equity returns. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were also scenting differences within the G7 as the U.S. commitment to a strong dollar is seemingly at odds with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's desire for a reversal in the euro. \par \par \uc2  Treasury Secretary Larry Summers last week loudly reaffirmed his commitment to a strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar moved in tight ranges against the yen on Monday to trade around 107.64/74 in late afternoon trade, off an early high of 108.14. But that was still well up from the 106.90 level held pre-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We want to see how serious the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in lending support to the euro, while the country's strong dollar policy stays in place," said Masayuki Yamamoto, assistant vice president at Bank of America in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  And that would require further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  DANISH REFERENDUM \par \par \uc2  A potential trigger for such an event is Thursday's Danish referendum on whether to join the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Euro bulls are hoping the G7's action and the bounce in the euro would win back some hearts and minds, and one opinion poll on Saturday did show the 'yes' camp closing on their opponents. \par \par \uc2  However, the latest poll released on Monday showed opponents of Denmark joining the euro led supporters by eight points. \par \par \uc2  Such an outcome would be a renewed blow to confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though dealers conceded they would be a lot more careful about shorting it now than just a few days ago. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9p06p16\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
