\par\par \b \uc2  REPEAT-WALL ST WEEKAHEAD-Street seen up on oil, euro relief. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Emma-Kate Symons \par \uc2 841 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2000\par 06:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 24 (Reuters) - The stock market this week is set to rise on expectations of lower oil prices and a higher euro, but fears of companies' warning profits will fall short of forecasts will linger. \par \par \uc2  Oil prices last week hit decade-highs amid strong global demand and short supplies, but eased after it became clear the U.S. government would release some of its strategic reserves to meet demand. At the same time, the U.S. dollar dropped against the euro at the end of the week after a joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the world's largest economies to prop up the value of the sagging European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The help in the short-run might help boost sentiment for equities," said Paul Cherney, market analyst S&P Marketscope. \par \par \uc2  The problem remains corporate warnings that profits will fall short of forecast due to the slowing U.S. economy. It is the tail-end of earnings warning season and investors are holding their collective breath, lest another market bellwether like Intel Corp. devastates global markets. \par \par \uc2  The world's largest chipmaker dragged down global stock indexes on Friday after it warned of lackluster revenue. But U.S. technology stocks, minus Intel, late Friday afternoon recouped most of their earlier losses, which bodes well for this week's market, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The reluctance to see follow-through on Friday to the downside is a positive backdrop for the Nasdaq," Cherney said. "We'll see jagged up-down trading for the first three days of this week, but it's going to try and move higher." \par \par \uc2  Investor worries persist about the strong U.S. dollar cutting into the profits of American multinationals. However, Friday's global \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of central banks from Europe, Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the ailing euro produced an impressive rally. \par \par \uc2  U.S. companies are expected to release quarterly earnings figures this week, include Internet equipment maker Cabletron Systems Inc. , hotel company Marriott International and Safeway Inc. . \par \par \uc2  New economic data to be released this week include consumer confidence and GDP figures. These data are expected to show a slowing but still strong U.S. economy, as most recent economic figures have. \par \par \uc2  Interest rates are not at the top of Wall Street's agenda because most market watchers believe the U.S. Federal Reserve is highly unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the lead-up to November's presidential election. EASING OIL PRICES, STRONGER EURO BODE WELL \par \par \uc2  Wall Street in recent weeks has been roiled by leading companies' warning profits will fall below forecasts because of higher energy prices and the weak euro, which cuts into export revenues. The worst news may be out of the bag, giving the stock market a reason to rise, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The markets for past two weeks have been discounting expected bad news during this confessional period and as that draws to a close in the next week, confidence is going to increase," Cherney said. "We've seen some pretty good signs here that downside is limited for the Nasdaq and the S&P." \par \par \uc2  Fears the strong U.S. dollar will shake the bottom-lines of top companies are expected to ease after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in concert with the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the beleaguered euro on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The unexpected boost had an immediate effect on the euro, which shot above 90 cents, up from its record low near 84.40 cents on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  But a firmer euro is a two-edged sword, Cherney said. \par \par \uc2  "Domestically, U.S. investors will be happy to feel that the negative impact of a weaker euro will have less weight on the bottom line of US multinationals," he said. "On the other hand, a strengthening domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually fosters investment in their (European) equity markets." \par \par \uc2  On Saturday, following a six-hour meeting, finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of 7 industrialised nations issued a strongly-worded statement indicating they were ready to act again in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and said it was crucial that oil producing countries take actions to reduce oil prices. \par \par \uc2  Oil prices, which had shot up to highs near $38 a barrel - not seen since the 1990-91 Gulf crisis - eased late Friday to end slightly below $33 in New York. Prices dropped slightly after President Bill Clinton released 30 billion barrels of oil from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve to ease worries that Americans will not have enough heating oil this winter. \par \par \uc2  "With high prices and more supply coming in the thinking is that it would drive the price closer to $30 in the future," said Richard Sichel, Chief Investment Officer at Philadelphia Trust Company. "I think there's a better tone to the market ... the euro and oil have both gone about as far as they're going to go in the wrong direction and we're looking for some good earnings in a few weeks, when the real season hits." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9p06o9k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
