\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro digs in,fear of G7 wars with market cynicism. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 861 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2000\par 05:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 25 (Reuters) - The euro kept a tentative grip on 88 cents in edgy trade Monday as fears of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  warred with the market's ingrained cynicism towards the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At midday the euro was digging in at $0.8807 having settled at $0.8758 late in New York on Friday after the Group of Seven's shock \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted it from $0.8620. \par \par \uc2  The industrialised powers backed up their assault in a statement over the weekend in which they pledged to monitor the markets closely and cooperate as appropriate. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Vice Finance Minister Toshiro Muto did his best to keep the market guessing on Monday, saying authorities are watching the euro carefully and will take action if needed. \par \par \uc2  All of which left dealers very wary of a repeat performance. \par \par \uc2  "It's obvious the G7 will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again - monetary authorities are particularly sensitive about their credibility so they're not likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  just once and go away," argued Ken Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Deutsche Bank. \par \par \uc2  BEWARE THE BOJ \par \par \uc2  Dealers were especially wary of the Bank of Japan since they were far more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  than their G7 colleagues and were believed to have been among the biggest buyers of euro on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "There's all sorts of guesses on how much they (the G7) bought, anywhere from 1.5 to 10 billion euros with the average around 3-5 billion," said a U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "We prefer the high side if only because 1.5 billion would be pocket change for the BOJ. They would do that alone and think nothing of it," he added. \par \par \uc2  He said Japan may have been restrained in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the euro in the past by a desire not to upset the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But now it was operating under the auspices of the G7 it might feel less inhibited. \par \par \uc2  "The talk is that the authorities would like to see euro/yen up at 100.00 for bookclosing since that would take some pressure off Japanese investors who have suffered big-time from the euro's fall," added the dealer. \par \par \uc2  Half-year bookclosing in Japan comes on Friday. \par \par \uc2  That thought helped keep the euro up at 94.90 yen against 94.98 in New York on Friday, despite sizeable selling from Japanese exporters. \par \par \uc2  The same exporters were also selling the dollar against the yen , pushing it back to 107.71 yen from an early high at 108.14, though that was still well up on the 106.90 level held pre-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  EVERYTHING NOT FORGIVEN \par \par \uc2  But the G7's protests did not mean the market had forgiven the euro for disappointing so often in the past or had given up its love affair with the dollar and U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, euro bears were highlighting the fact that Treasury Secretary Larry Summers had loudly reaffirmed his commitment to a strong dollar policy. This, they argued, showed that the U.S. was less than fully committed to further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We want to see how serious the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in lending support to the euro, while the country's strong dollar policy stays in place," said Masayuki Yamamoto, assistant vice president at Bank of America in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  And that would require further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A potential trigger for such an event is Thursday's Danish referendum on whether to join the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Euro bulls are hoping the G7's action and the bounce in the euro would win back some hearts and minds, and one opinion poll on Saturday did show the 'yes' camp closing on their opponents. \par \par \uc2  However, a separate poll released on Sunday showed the naysayers extending their lead to 11 percentage points leaving the market betting on a 'No' vote. \par \par \uc2  Such an outcome would be a renewed blow to confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though dealers conceded they would be a lot more careful about shorting it now than just a few days ago. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0312 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab      Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   1999 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab   22 Sep.\par \tab \tab \tab      -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr\tab \tab   0.8809   0.8758   +0.58   -12.52   1.0070\par  Dlr/yen\tab \tab    107.72   107.90   -0.17    +5.39   102.21\par  Euro/yen\tab \tab    94.95    94.98   -0.03    -7.90   103.10\par  Dlr/swiss\tab \tab  1.7268   1.7328   -0.35    +8.59   1.5902\par  Stg/dlr\tab \tab    1.4591   1.4524   +0.46    -9.80   1.6176\par  Can/dlr\tab \tab    1.4883   1.4880   +0.02    +2.96   1.4455\par  Aus/dlr\tab \tab    0.5452   0.5440   +0.22   -16.93   0.6563\par  Euro/swiss\tab \tab 1.5215   1.5196   +0.13    -5.12   1.6036\par  Euro/stg\tab \tab   0.6043   0.6023   +0.33    -3.14   0.6239\par     ..\tab   Debt...\tab Indicators...\par \f28 \par \par \b \