\par\par \b \uc2  Euro shoots higher after joint  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 151 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 13:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 22 (Reuters) - The euro shot higher against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was joined in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros by the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japan a day before the Group of Seven industrial nations met. \par \par \uc2  It was the first time since 1995 that the U.S., European, and Japanese have been spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in concert on the foreign exchanges. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosted the euro more than five percent on the day against the dollar, pushing it above $0.90 for the first time since September 4. \par \par \uc2  The ECB said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been launched because of concern about the potential implications of recent moves in the euro. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen to record lows in recent weeks against the dollar, the yen, and the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  It had lost nearly 30 percent against the dollar since its launch. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9m06zd7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
