\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-G7 defends euro in run-up to Prague meeting. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alister Bull \par \uc2 574 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 14:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  PRAGUE, Sept 22 (Reuters) - Central banks from Europe, Japan and the United States stepped into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Friday to defend the battered euro on the eve of a top-level meeting of the Group of Seven industrialised countries. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the first joint \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action since 1995, drove the euro up sharply, although the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  later gave up some of these gains as financial markets fought back. \par \par \uc2  Ministers from the world's richest countries are gathering in Prague for the annual meetings of World Bank and International Monetary Fund. Several officials confirmed their central banks had stepped into the markets. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which launched the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January 1999, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had taken place because of concern about the potential implications of recent moves in the euro, which tumbled to record lows this week. \par \par \uc2  The low point below $0.85 was almost 30 percent below the euro's level against the dollar at the launch. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounced back above $.90 after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but then gave up some of these gains to trade just below $0.89. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets were surprised that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place before the G7 meeting, which will be attended by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs and finance ministers from Britain, Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Nobody was expecting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the meeting over the weekend," said Lorenze Codogno, of Bank of America in London. "It's very difficult to say now if it will be successful... If we see the euro stabilising well above the level that prevailed this week, it means the market is convinced now there will be a sea change." \par \par \uc2  The euro and the soaring oil price had been seen at the top of the G7's agenda, but markets had doubted the chances of a 'Prague Accord' sanctioning \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lift the euro. \par \par \uc2  Bad vibes on the euro had also damaged Danish chances of a yes vote in a September 28 referendum to join the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - latest polls put the 'no' camp slightly ahead. \par \par \uc2  And a few U.S. officials have openly questioned whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will survive. \par \par \uc2  But European Union officials shot down the notion that the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , used by 11 members of the European Union but not in the pockets of citizens until January 2002, was in any way negotiable. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets do not appear to be pricing in the possibility the single curency could unravel and European leaders, who have invested huge personal political capital in getting the euro off the ground, want to keep it that way. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg last week said there was little chance of a Plaza-style accord, which 15 years ago unleashed concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to quell the dollar, or the 1987 Louvre Accord which did the reverse. \par \par \uc2  The G7 will meet in the German embassy in Prague's historic centre, well away from the conference centre occupied by the IMF which activists have threatened to place under seige. \par \par \uc2  Events begin with a 0600 GMT breakfast hosted by German Finance Minister Hans Eichel with the formal G7 meeting scheduled to start at 1000 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The G7 gathering is scheduled to last up to five hours with press briefings pencilled in from 1530 GMT, and the release of the G7's formal communique expected from that time onwards. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9m06zd3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
