\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  strategists see uphill struggle for euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lida Poletz \par \uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 21:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 22 (Reuters) - The battle to shore up the euro was joined on Friday with a lightning strike of Group of Seven (G7) buying, but U.S. analysts said central banks face a tough fight to turn around the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's interminable slump. \par \par \uc2  Simply pitting central banks against the market will not automatically remove the main reason for euro weakness - namely that investors find U.S. assets far more appealing than those in Europe. \par \par \uc2  "It's not enough yet to change bearish sentiment," Jim McCormick, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan, said of the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which caught markets off guard coming just before this weekend's meeting of G7 finance ministers. \par \par \uc2  "Certainly policy makers have drawn a line in the sand and tried to turn the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around, but they're still sitting in front of a wall of money," McCormick said. \par \par \uc2  And from a purely technical perspective, technical analysts said the euro faces stiff resistance to staying above 90 cents, and may need to retrace all its gains before building a base and moving higher. \par \par \uc2  MORE WORK NEEDED \par \par \uc2  The euro hurtled up more than 5 percent against the dollar early on Friday as central banks from Europe, the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japan united in a concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros, pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  temporarily above 90 cents. \par \par \uc2  But the euro pared much of those gains, falling below 88 cents in early U.S. afternoon trade in the absence of further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying. \par \par \uc2  Chartists said the euro will be hard-pressed to reverse its steep downtrend with just one spike higher. \par \par \uc2  The beleaguered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shed close to 30 percent of its value against the dollar over the past 20 months, falling to a record low near 84.40 cents on Wednesday after being launched with fanfare at $1.17 in January 1999. \par \par \uc2  "The euro has been going down for such a long time its going to take a while to build a base, and I'm not sure if one day is going to be it," Robert Zukowski, technical analyst at 4Cast Ltd. \par \par \uc2  "Technically at some point, probably over the next couple of weeks, we are going to digest what we did today and maybe retest the low 84 area and try to firm up a base." \par \par \uc2  Going forward the euro faces strong resistance around 90.50 cents, which was an area of congestion last month, technical analysts said. But the euro may have to push even higher, above 97 cents - a level last seen in June - to really prove it is on the mend. \par \par \uc2  The vulnerability of the euro's rebound was reflected in a snap Reuters poll of economists in North America and Europe, who predicted the that the euro would be a 89 cents a month from now. \par \par \uc2  DOLLARS ON THE CHEAP \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that for many European companies that need dollars to finance their acquisition of U.S. businesses, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  simply provided a nice opportunity to buy dollars on the cheap. \par \par \uc2  Speculators, meanwhile, will view the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action as a challenge. \par \par \uc2  "The market will definitely try to test their resolve, see if they really, really want to hold this up," said Jan Soulyeman, senior trader at Credit Agricole Indosuez. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said all eyes were now on the weekend G7 meeting of finance ministers in Prague. The ministers were expected to issue a post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  communique on the euro. \par \par \uc2  The strength of the statement's wording, which should signal the likelihood of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the days ahead, may decide whether the euro sinks or swims, traders said. \par \par \uc2  In the options market, traders said implied volatility in the most short-dated options had surged sharply after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and stayed firm even as the euro pared gains. Traders said that meant the market was still very jittery about the outcome of the G7 meeting and wary of further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. \par \par \uc2  (additional reporting by David Bailey). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9m06ldb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
