\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 2-G7 rallies behind euro after "Versailles Accord". \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alister Bull, European Economics Correspondent \par \uc2 858 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 18:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  PRAGUE, Sept 22 (Reuters) - The world's richest nations on Friday unleashed their central banks against \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to boost the bloodied euro, with the unexpected participation of the United States taking markets by surprise. \par \par \uc2  On the 15th anniversary of the Plaza Accord and just one day before finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven (G7) industrialised countries meet in Prague, central banks from Europe, Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought euros. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ECB's first since its birth 20 months ago and the first action involving the U.S., Japanese and European central banks ince 1995, drove the euro up sharply to above $0.90 at one stage. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  later gave up some of these gains after comments from U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which launched the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January 1999, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had taken place because of concern about the potential implications of recent moves in the euro, which tumbled to record lows this week. \par \par \uc2  Officials said that the decision to act had been taken by European finance ministers at a meeting two weeks ago in Versailles, outside Paris. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. said they joined at the request of the ECB. Japan separately said that their shared concern on the impact of a weak euro on the world economy had prompted participation. \par \par \uc2  U.S. CATCHES MARKET OFF GUARD \par \par \uc2  Markets had judged that the U.S. would not join action to aid the euro and news they were on board took them by surprise although this impact was blunted after Summers spoke. \par \par \uc2  He said Washington stood by its policy that a strong dollar was in the national interest - words that puished the euro back under $0.88, although it remained well above levels prior to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Italian Treasury Minister Vincenzo Visco said, "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , puts into action the decisions taken by EU finance ministers in Versailles, and subsequently supported by the (bank) governors, to strengthen the euro in the interests of Europe." The euro's decline has been widely blamed as a factor behind rising European inflation rates, with this year's surge in dollar-denominated oil prices exaggerating the impact. \par \par \uc2  The low point for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week was below $0.85 and almost 30 percent below the euro's level against the dollar at the launch. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said the action had been spurred by concern over high world oil prices and left open the option for further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Of course at G7 we'll be discussing the market's reaction, but I cannot comment whether we will take further action," he said. \par \par \uc2  TIMING OF STRIKE ADMIRED \par \par \uc2  Financial markets were surprised that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place before the G7 meeting, which will be attended by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs and finance ministers from Britain, Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Nobody was expecting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the meeting over the weekend," said Lorenzo Codogno, of Bank of America in London. \par \par \uc2  "It's very difficult to say now if it will be successful... If we see the euro stabilising well above the level that prevailed this week, it means the market is convinced now there will be a sea change," he added. \par \par \uc2  The euro and the soaring oil price had been seen at the top of the G7's agenda, but markets had doubted the chances of a 'Prague Accord' sanctioning \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lift the euro. \par \par \uc2  Many analysts had assumed the U.S. would be reluctant to publicly sell dollars before November's presidential election. \par \par \uc2  "It's changed the market's perception of the U.S. approach," said Alison Cottrell, economist at PaineWebber International in London. "That's the big surprise factor." \par \par \uc2  Bad vibes on the euro had also damaged Danish chances of a yes vote in a September 28 referendum to join the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - latest polls put the 'no' camp slightly ahead. \par \par \uc2  And a few U.S. monetary officials have openly questioned whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will survive as a project. \par \par \uc2  But European Union officials shot down the notion that the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , used by 11 members of the European Union but not in the pockets of citizens until January 2002, was in any way negotiable. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg last week said there was little chance of a Plaza-style accord, which 15 years ago unleashed concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to quell the dollar, or the 1987 Louvre Accord which did the reverse. \par \par \uc2  The G7 will meet in the German embassy in Prague's historic centre, well away from the conference centre occupied by the IMF which activists have threatened to place under seige. \par \par \uc2  Events begin with a 0600 GMT breakfast hosted by German Finance Minister Hans Eichel with the formal G7 meeting scheduled to start at 1000 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The G7 gathering is scheduled to last up to five hours with press briefings pencilled in from 1530 GMT, and the release of the G7's formal communique expected from that time onwards. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9m06kg6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
