\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Federal Reserve joins  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  to support euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Knut Engelmann \par \uc2 342 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 14:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Sept 22 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday it had joined Japan and Europe to buy euros on world \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , defying market expectations that it would not step in. \par \par \uc2  "At the initiative of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  the monetary authorities of the United States and Japan joined with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in exchange markets because of their shared concern about the potential implications of recent movements in the euro for the world economy," a Treasury spokeswoman told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on the eve of a meeting of the Group of Seven industrialised nations in Prague, pushed the euro up more than 5 percent on the day against the dollar, sending it above $0.90 for the first time since September 4. The euro gave up some ground and was trading at $0.89 later. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said earlier on Friday that U.S. and Japanese authorities had joined it in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of concern over the potential implications of recent euro moves. \par \par \uc2  Analysts believed the fact that the central banks acted in concert sent a positive signal to euro markets. They said the big surprise was the U.S. willingness to join the effort. \par \par \uc2  "It's changed the market's perception of the U.S. approach," said Allison Cottrell of PaineWebber International in London. "That's the big surprise factor." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Treasury did not specify how much it spent on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but said it used its own funds for the action, a Treasury official said. The official did not spell out why the United States decided to help prop up the floundering \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers was due to hold a news conference later on Friday in Washington. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen to record lows in recent weeks against the dollar, the yen and the Swiss franc and had lost nearly 30 percent against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since its launch. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9m06jve\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
