\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS - Intel hits tech shares, euro rallies. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kristin Roberts \par \uc2 724 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 18:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (previous LONDON) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 22 (Reuters) - Technology shares rocked Wall Street on Friday after computer-chip giant Intel Corp. warned an already-nervous market that revenues would fall short of expectations. \par \par \uc2  European shares, however, pared their losses from an earlier Intel-inspired pullback after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  coordinated a surprise \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost the beleaguered euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had dropped to record lows against the U.S. dollar this week. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own would be fine (for equities markets), but for Intel," said Arthur Hogan, chief market analyst at Jefferies & Co. A healthier single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would tend to bolster stocks, as the slumping euro has been blamed in recent weeks for shortfalls in U.S. corporate profits. \par \par \uc2  "The combined effect is, well, the euro issue is being overridden by Intel," Hogan said. "Intel's problem has to do with demand and that's a major disappointment." \par \par \uc2  The technology-driven Nasdaq composite index lost more than 5 percent at the opening bell but managed to climb from those lows. The gauge settled down to a loss of 112.39 points, or 2.94 percent, at 3,716.48. \par \par \uc2  Intel was the most actively traded stock on Wall Street, dropping about 13 points to $48-11/16 with more than 135 million shares trading hands within the first hour of trade. Volume was approaching 190 million shares by late morning. \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average .DJI) fell 96.10 points, or 0.87 percent, to 10,670.38. Intel, one of the 30 Dow components, accounted for 77 points of that decline while financial, drug and consumer products stocks gained. \par \par \uc2  The widely tracked Standard & Poor's 500 index fell 15.25 points, or 1.05 percent, to 1,433.66. \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the euro surged after falling near its lifetime low against the dollar at 84.40 cents on Wednesday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose to a high above 90 cents but pared some of its gains to trade around 88.50 cents, strongly above the previous day's U.S. close near 86 cents. \par \par \uc2  "In the war, they've captured good territory, but the question is, can they hold on?" said Marc Chandler, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Mellon Bank in New York. "The market has sold the euro for 20 months. On balance you have to believe they still want to be sellers." \par \par \uc2  The euro also was sharply higher against the yen, hitting a peak above 95.50 yen compared to the previous close near 91.5 yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro-buying spree was spearheaded by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which coordinated an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Britain, Japan, Canada and France that boosted the euro more than 5 percent against the dollar. That move was the first joint \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action involving the U.S. Federal Reserve, the Bank of Japan and European central banks since 1995. \par \par \uc2  U.S. bond prices moved higher as sliding stock markets sparked a flight to treasuries, overriding the negative impact of the euro \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Bonds normally tend to move lower after \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as money flows away from U.S. Treasuries toward the rallying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Trading was volatile as equity, oil and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets competed for investor attention. \par \par \uc2  In morning trading, benchmark 10-year notes rose 6/32 to 99-14/32, yielding 5.83 percent. Thirty-year bonds gained 10/32 to 104-26/32, yielding 5.90 percent. \par \par \uc2  London's FTSE 100 was down 0.08 percent at 6,194.0 while Paris's CAC-40 gauge fell 24.48 points, or 0.39 percent, to 6,230.29. \par \par \uc2  STMicroelectronics, Europe's number one chipmaker, lost 2.46 percent, while while Germany's Infineon shed 5.29 percent. But cyclicals, media and financial services shares all fell more than one percent as well, as investors sought safety in bond markets and cash. \par \par \uc2  Asian markets took Intel's announcement hard, with Tokyo's Nikkei 225 share index falling three percent and the Hong Kong Hang Seng down 3.6 percent. \par \par \uc2  World oil prices, another source of worry to investors, stabilised after losses prompted by speculation over a pending U.S. plan to allow a rare release of some of its emergency stockpiles to ensure plentiful supplies this winter. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9m06fbx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
