\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Summers says U.S. still favors strong dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Knut Engelmann \par \uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 17:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Sept 22 (Reuters) - Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers insisted on Friday that U.S. policy favouring a strong dollar was unchanged, despite a decision to join the first-ever global rescue mission to boost Europe's sagging single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Our policy on the dollar is unchanged," Summers said at a news conference hours after the coordinated global \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , by the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other top industrial nations, to sell their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the euro and so push up its value. \par \par \uc2  "As I have said many times, a strong dollar is in the national interest of the United States," he said, repeating a refrain that he and his predecessor Robert Rubin made familiar but one that had been dropped from Summers' more recent comments on the topic. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on the eve of a meeting of top finance officials from the Group of Seven industrialised nations in Prague, pushed the euro up more than 5 percent against the dollar on the day, but it quickly lost some of the gains. \par \par \uc2  Summers declined to comment further on the move, or to detail how much money the Federal Reserve had spent at Treasury's instruction on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The statement speaks for itself," he said, referring to a one-paragraph Treasury statement earlier in the day which confirmed the global \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and cited concern over the euro's effect on the world economy. \par \par \uc2  "It is not our practice to evaluate \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not taken part in any concerted efforts to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since mid-1998, when it helped the Bank of Japan to sell dollars to push up the value of the then-weak yen. \par \par \uc2  U.S. officials stressed that the initiative for Friday's joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came from Europe, and emphasised that the Fed went into the markets only once the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had set the ball rolling. Sources indicated the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been talked about as early as Thursday evening in Washington. \par \par \uc2  OIL WORRIES \par \par \uc2  Summers, preparing to leave for the Prague meetings, also said recent oil price surges were "a concern for consumers and businesses and around the world" and posed a "real risk" for the U.S. economy. Oil would be high on the agenda at the Prague talks, he said. \par \par \uc2  "More stable prices, in line with the historic norms, are in the mutual interest of both oil producers and consumers," Summers said. But he declined to specify a price target. \par \par \uc2  His comments came a day after President Bill Clinton's aides appeared to unite behind plans to release some oil from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve (SPR) to try to lower the highest oil prices in a decade. \par \par \uc2  Summers, who had opposed the large-scale release of SPR oil in a confidential memo just last week, said on Thursday such a move could be appropriate. \par \par \uc2  "This is a situation that is rapidly changing and we have always recognised that this is a situation that needs to be monitored as conditions change and that we need to be prepared to take whatever actions will be most constructive in light of changing circumstances," Summers said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Commenting on economic growth elsewhere in the world, Summer said there were "signs of stronger economic growth in all of the major industrialised countries," but added reforms still were needed to boost investment. \par \par \uc2  He said the need for stepped-up reforms was "recognized as a priority" by European leaders, while Japan was aware of the need for further steps to boost demand in its economy. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9m06faw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
