\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 4-U.S. joins euro rescue, stuns world markets. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Knut Engelmann \par \uc2 724 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 19:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Sept 22 (Reuters) - The United States on Friday took world markets by surprise by joining the first-ever global effort to prop up Europe's sagging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but insisted its own preference for a strong dollar had not changed a bit. \par \par \uc2  "Our policy on the dollar is unchanged," U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers said at a news conference only hours after the coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  among industrial countries, in which they sold their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the euro and boost its value. \par \par \uc2  "As I have said many times, a strong dollar is in the national interest of the United States," he said, repeating a mantra made famous by his predecessor Robert Rubin and repeated many times by Summers himself - until more recently, when the stock phrase had been dropped from his \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  vocabulary. \par \par \uc2  Coming on the eve of a meeting of top finance officials from the Group of Seven major nations in Prague and just weeks ahead of a U.S. presidential election, the move took markets by surprise - a wholly desired effect - and managed to briefly boost the euro by some five percent against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But the hapless \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  quickly gave back some of the gains and was last trading at just below $0.88 - still about 25 percent below the level it was introduced at last year. \par \par \uc2  "It caught everyone off guard," said Jeffrey Yu, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Sanwa Bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  Summers, who has long been reluctant to join any effort to help the euro, declined to comment on the move's effectiveness. "It is not our practice to evaluate \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not taken part in any concerted efforts to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since June 1998, when it helped the Bank of Japan sell dollars and buy yen to push up the value of the then-weak yen against the backdrop of a worsening economic crisis in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Summers declined to say how much the Federal Reserve had spent on Friday at Treasury's direction to buy euros. \par \par \uc2  But the fact that the Fed actually used its own funds - rather than acting as an agent for other central banks - was seen in financial markets as signalling a growing, if still tentative, U.S. commitment to the euro which was introduced with great fanfare in Europe in January 1999. \par \par \uc2  NO PROMISES MADE \par \par \uc2  Summers, echoing statements by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japan, said the euro's sharp decline had raised concerns about "potential implications" for the entire world economy. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp plunge against both the dollar and the yen has undermined earnings of U.S. companies operating in Europe, who have seen the value of their foreign profits eroded once they are converted back into dollars. \par \par \uc2  Still, U.S. officials insisted the initiative for Friday's joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had come from Europe, and stressed that the Fed went into the markets only once the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had set the ball rolling. Sources indicated the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been discussed in Washington as early as Thursday evening. \par \par \uc2  But unlike two years ago, when Japan promised a raft of measures to boost its then-recessionary economy in return for U.S. help on the yen, Washington did not appear to have received any firm promises by European leaders that they will do whatever they can to boost their own economy. \par \par \uc2  "It's very doubtful the United States got anything in return," said Tom Gallagher of political analysis firm ISI Group. "A sustained turnaround in the euro will await improvement in the growth prospects (in Europe)." \par \par \uc2  While expressing his satisfaction over the improved outlook for Europe's economy - which he has said repeatedly is the key determinant for the euro's strength or weakness - Summers indicated governments there still had their work cut out for them. \par \par \uc2  "Continuing structural reforms in Europe to increase productive potential, to raise incentives for job-creating investments, are recognised as priorities by European leaders," he said. \par \par \uc2  Summers was due to meet his European counterparts in Prague early Saturday in a series of private meetings ahead of the actual G7 gathering, which will be attended by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  heads and finance ministers from the United States, Japan, Britain, Canada, France, Germany and Italy. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9m06eww\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
