\par\par \b \uc2  Feeble euro seen needing more than words at G7. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mariko Hayashibara \par \uc2 678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2000\par 08:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 21 (Reuters) - Recent Group of Seven (G7) meetings have usually found Japan seeking to control a troublesome \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but this time the onus is on the Europeans to stop the euro's incredible shrinking act. \par \par \uc2  Analysts in Tokyo say Europe's options are also shrinking fast, and hold out little hope that the weekend's meeting of finance ministers in Prague will reverse the slide. \par \par \uc2  Markets are looking for signs in Prague that the rich world's patience with the euro's relentless slide - its value has fallen by 28 percent over its 20-month life - has finally snapped. But with Europe apparently having few tools of its own to arrest the decline and Washington unwilling to weaken its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an election year, the omens are not good for the bedraggled euro, which has hit a series of record lows this week. \par \par \uc2  The situation is an unusual reversal of roles for G7 members more used to dealing with Japanese appeals to help weaken its robust yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts in Tokyo agree the most realistic way to put some spine back in the euro is through joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in conjunction with the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japan. \par \par \uc2  But while Japan may be willing, the U.S. administration is highly unlikely to risk puncturing its strong-dollar credentials so close to November's presidential elections. And without the Federal Reserve, Tokyo is likely to get cold feet about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , leaving the ECB with few realistic options, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  "Merely a supportive word, or ECB solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't help the euro much," said Ryohei Muramatsu, manager at Commerzbank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Fading expectations of meaningful G7 action, fuelled by a perceived lack of consensus among European leaders, have pulled the euro to repeated record lows this week. In Thursday trade in Tokyo, it was hovering just above those levels at around $0.8471 and at 90.30 against the yen. \par \par \uc2  OPTIONS LIMITED \par \par \uc2  The euro's fall, together with the oil-price surge, has sparked serious worries of inflation in some quarters. \par \par \uc2  The obvious solution of hiking rates is seen as risky at a time when doubts are mounting that the German economy may be leveling off. \par \par \uc2  This problem was underlined on Wednesday when the influential Ifo institute's West German business climax index fell for the third month in a row, while Italy posted slower-than-expected economic growth. \par \par \uc2  Even data on Wednesday showing the U.S. trade deficit mushrooming to $31.89 billion in July offered only fleeting aid to the euro as dealers and analysts believe Washington needs a strong dollar to keep funding the shortfall. \par \par \uc2  Just a few days ahead of the G7 meeting, U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers reiterated his support for the strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the United States really cares much about other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the moment," Muramatsu said, adding that the weak euro hasn't yet done serious enough damage to U.S. firms. \par \par \uc2  This is not true for Japan, whose investors' hefty euro assets have been pummeled by the euro's fall, but analysts say the recent surge in oil prices has likely made Tokyo more appreciative of the strong yen by helping it keep import costs down. \par \par \uc2  There are even worries that a strong expression of G7 concern about the euro could have the very opposite effect, by deepening the confidence crisis engulfing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The chances are the Europeans won't take a risk now by stating a strong concern about the euro. It may just give the impression the euro is a failure," said Shinichi Sato, manager at Tokyo-Mitsubishi Securities' Market Research Division. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say any indication of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would give the euro a lift, but that its descent could resume if words are not followed by action. \par \par \uc2  "The euro's recent decline is actually largely in line with fundamentals, something contrary to what the ECB officials are saying," Commerzbank's Muramatsu said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9l070cj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
