\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro takes flight as bears bellow G7 challenge. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 837 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2000\par 08:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 19 (Reuters) - The euro sundered major support at 85 cents on Tuesday to suffer its seventh record low in just 10 sessions, as the prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reverse its slump remained faint. \par \par \uc2  In late trade here the euro had crumbled to $0.8491 having changed hands as low as $0.8480 compared to Monday's all-too-brief all-time low of $0.8510. \par \par \uc2  It also hit a record low on the yen around 90.60 yen so surpassing the previous trough of 90.70 reached on September 12. \par \par \uc2  Dealers did report a crumb of caution as this weekend's meeting of Group of Seven Finance Ministers approached, bringing with it the usual talk of supportive statements or even coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials, long concerned at the euro's weakness against the yen, tried hard to keep the market wary. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa on Tuesday said the euro was sure to be on the agenda at the meeting and Japan's stance was to cooperate to achieve exchange rate stability. \par \par \uc2  But traders said bears were unbowed, arguing that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would only work if the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  joined in and the prospects of that seemed remote at best. \par \par \uc2  "We're comfortable being short the euro because there's no way the U.S. will risk hurting confidence in the dollar when there's an election near and a huge deficit to fund," said a European bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "Even Welteke admitted as much," he added. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke conceded the it would be difficult for the Americans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an election year. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas President Robert McTeer overnight suggested the euro's weakness may be of no concern at all, saying it seemed to be stimulating eurozone economic growth. \par \par \uc2  And to top it off, a wire report quoted influential investment advisor Richard Medley saying that while the United States did have some concerns about the euro, it was unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  drew scant support from suspicions the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been buying on Monday as part of its plan to convert the interest on its reserves into euro. \par \par \uc2  "The ECB bought twice in European time, lifting the euro as high as $0.8575, but each time the impact was depressingly brief from their point of view," said a U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "If they dreamt up this scheme as an alternative to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they must be dismayed at the outcome," he added. \par \par \uc2  One potential plus for the euro could be the recent slide in U.S. equities which, ironically, was in part caused by the euro's weakness eating in corporate earnings there. \par \par \uc2  The Nasdaq shed 2.8 percent on Monday while the Dow lost 1.08 percent to hit a six-week low. \par \par \uc2  Longer-dated Treasury prices also extended their sharp losses, stirring speculation investors could start cutting their exposure to the U.S. altogether if this trend continued. \par \par \uc2  However, traders were sceptical, noting U.S. interest rates still offered an attractive premium over the euro and nowhere was safe if U.S. markets suffered. \par \par \uc2  "You look around the world and you have to ask where else is there to invest than the States?" said the U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "Take oil prices for instance. Sure they're bad for the U.S. but they're even worse for Europe and Asia," he said, noting that Asian share markets had taken a beating in recent days. \par \par \uc2  "And when funds shift out of Asia there's usually only one safe-haven of choice: America," he added. \par \par \uc2  Which was one reason the dollar had recently risen on most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and reached record highs on the Australian and New Zealand dollars. \par \par \uc2  One \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  it could not dominate was the yen, which was kept strong by Japan's massive trade surplus and the ingrained reluctance of domestic investors to send money abroad. \par \par \uc2  In late trade the dollar was sitting at 106.84 yen having spent the entire day in a snug 106.81/107.10 range. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0606 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab      Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   1999 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab   18 Sep.\par \tab \tab \tab      -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr\tab \tab   0.8508   0.8533   -0.29   -15.51   1.0070\par  Dlr/yen\tab \tab    106.83   106.89   -0.06    +4.52   102.21\par  Euro/yen\tab \tab    90.89    91.21   -0.35   -11.84   103.10\par  Dlr/swiss\tab \tab  1.7878   1.7830   +0.27   +12.43   1.5902\par  Stg/dlr\tab \tab    1.4043   1.4056   -0.09   -13.19   1.6176\par  Can/dlr\tab \tab    1.4869   1.4872   -0.02    +2.86   1.4455\par  Aus/dlr\tab \tab    0.5433   0.5419   +0.26   -17.22   0.6563\par  Euro/swiss\tab \tab 1.5217   1.5235   -0.12    -5.11   1.6036\par  Euro/stg\tab \tab   0.6055   0.6068   -0.21    -2.95   0.6239.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9j07fr8\par }\page {\p