\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Miyazawa says Japan ready to help on weak euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By William Mallard \par \uc2 580 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2000\par 09:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 19 (Reuters) - Japan signalled on Tuesday it is ready to help European nations stabilise the sagging euro but said it was unclear what, if any, action the industrial powers might take. \par \par \uc2  "I want to hear what the other ministers have to say, but fundamentally, Japan will cooperate to achieve overall \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability," said Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa, referring to a meeting this weekend of finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven club in Prague. \par \par \uc2  "As to whether we actually do anything or not, we will respond flexibly," Miyazawa said. \par \par \uc2  The head of the ministry's International Bureau, Zembei Mizoguchi, said Japan shares Europe's worries about the euro, which has repeatedly plumbed record lows against the dollar and yen in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  Referring to comments by German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Ernst Welteke that the euro's drop was not justified by economic fundamentals, Mizoguchi said: "That has been said by many in Europe. Concerns over the euro have been expressed in a statement by European finance ministers as well. We hold the same view." \par \par \uc2  JAPAN FEELS EUROPE'S PAIN \par \par \uc2  But Miyazawa expressed puzzlement on where the Europeans in fact stand on the euro, and left unstated was whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  - generally seen as sceptical toward market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - would join any action. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa quoted European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg as having previously said a weak euro was not a problem as long as price stability could be maintained. But the Japanese finance minister added that recently surging oil prices may have changed the view of European authorities. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan (BOJ) Governor Masaru Hayami said later on Tuesday he would seek to ask his European counterparts at the Prague meeting for the reason for the euro's fall. \par \par \uc2  "I'm sure there are various political, economic factors as well as direct investment flows out of Europe, but I cannot be sure why the euro is falling," Hayami told a regular news conference. \par \par \uc2  The euro sundered major support at 85 cents in Tokyo trade on Tuesday to suffer its seventh record low in just 10 sessions. G7 finance ministers are set to discuss its fall this weekend, but few expect them to agree to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behalf. \par \par \uc2  The European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a fresh record low of $0.8491. \par \par \uc2  G7 TO MULL EURO, OIL \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa said the euro and oil prices would surely be a G7 topic. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa said G7 finance ministers were considering whether to express an opinion on oil prices. But he indicated unanimity may be hard to find as the effect of the oil price rise varies greatly across the G7. Demonstrations seeking petroleum tax cuts and gas station queues, coupled with the weak euro, have inflamed the problem in Europe, he said. \par \par \uc2  BOJ's Hayami cited a strong yen as a factor helping to blunt the impact of rising oil prices on the Japanese economy, although he believed that high oil prices would continue for a while. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Yoshiro Mori said on Tuesday he was worried about the oil price surge but saw no need yet to dip into the government's stockpile. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, October crude oil on the New York Mercantile Exchange rose to a new high of $37.15 per barrel. \par \par \uc2  Oil prices are the highest since the 1990-1991 Gulf war after Iraq invaded fellow OPEC member Kuwait. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9j078c8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
