\par\par \b \uc2  Frank Fed words on euro dim hopes of US aid. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lida Poletz \par \uc2 712 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2000\par 00:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 18 (Reuters) - A brace of unusually frank words about the sagging euro from U.S. monetary officials are bolstering the market's conviction that Europe will have to deal with its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slump without Washington's help. \par \par \uc2  The remarks - from Federal Reserve Bank presidents Alfred Broaddus of Richmond and Robert McTeer of Dallas - have had special resonance in the market ahead of a weekend Group of Seven ministers' meeting, analysts said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Although they should probably not be construed as policy statements, analysts said, the Fed officials' remarks do highlight the view that Europe's worries about its single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are not shared as intensely by U.S. officials. \par \par \uc2  "It's certainly not helpful. It doesn't give the market any confidence that they should expect anything new," said Robert Sinche, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  McTeer said in an interview with Reuters on Monday that the euro's weakness may actually be helping the euro zone's economic recovery - even if it hurts European pride. \par \par \uc2  His comments followed unusual bluntness on Friday from Broaddus, who startled markets by saying the euro's decline raised questions about its longer-term survival. This prompted traders to bully the beleaguered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down to fresh record lows against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  EURO PAIN IS EUROPE'S PROBLEM \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the statements appear to reinforce what the market already suspects - that the United States will be reluctant to take a strong stand on supporting the euro at the G7 finance ministers' meeting on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  The euro, which hit fresh lifetime lows against the dollar near 85 cents on Monday, has lost over 25 percent of its value since it was launched 20 months ago. \par \par \uc2  "The Europeans want the U.S. to agree to some threatening comment about the euro in the G7 statement, and what McTeer is probably trying to do is hint publicly that the Europeans just have to relax," said Paul Podolsky, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Fleet Capital Markets in Boston. \par \par \uc2  "The reality is that the U.S. is likely to agree to some sort of very vague statement that exchange rates should reflect the long-term value for the economy." \par \par \uc2  McTeer, asked if he was concerned about the euro's sharp decline, told Reuters: "All that could happen is it could go down further," adding that its weakness seemed to be stimulating the euro zone economies. \par \par \uc2  McTeer's comments, which elicited no reaction in U.S. trade, reflected recent remarks from German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder, who said earlier this month that a weak euro was a source of satisfaction as it had helped German exports. But after the euro slid on his comments, Schroeder backtracked strenuously, saying he supports a strong euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  UNLIKELY \par \par \uc2  Even less likely is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  will agree to concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the euro - something analysts say is the only hope for turning around the euro's fortunes quickly. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Treasury has reiterated in recent days its policy of favouring a strong dollar, which helps keep down U.S. inflation and interest rates. Beyond that, U.S. officials are seen as reluctant to toy with monetary policy ahead of the presidential election in November, \par \par \uc2  That comes as no surprise to anyone in the market, not least of which are European monetary officials. Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke, asked whether the U.S. could be persuaded into joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help the euro, said: "We're in an election year and that makes me think it would be pretty difficult to get a clear position from the Americans." \par \par \uc2  Still, some analysts stressed that the Fed officials may simply not be aware of how sensitive markets have been to talk about the euro - for instance, the sell-off that followed Broaddus' comments that euro weakness "raises questions about viability and the longer-run success of the euro." \par \par \uc2  "I would be extremely surprised if these were policy statements," Citibank's Sinche said. "They're answers to questions about current events, and I would say they're independent comments that are unrelated to views that Mr. (Fed Chairman Alan) Greenspan might have or that the Treasury might have." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9i07e3l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
