\par\par \b \uc2  IMM  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  lower early, euro rally dies. \b0\par\par\uc2 600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2000\par 16:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, Sept 14 (Reuters) - December euro futures initially bounced higher on Thursday after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced plans for what traders said was a "roundabout" form of propping up the struggling euro, but by midmorning all IMM \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures contracts were in negative territory. \par \par \uc2  December euro futures had jumped more than one cent higher overnight just after an announcement from the ECB that it would sell the interest income from its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and buy euros. Recent ECB data said central banks in the euro zone held about 260.9 billion euros in reserve as of September 8. \par \par \uc2  ECB President Wim Duisenberg said at an ECB news conference early in the session that a total of "2.5 billion euros will be sold in a very short time and this exercise will be repeated relatively frequently in the future." \par \par \uc2  The December euro contract traded near the lows of the session range early Thursday after paring gains on comments from Duisenberg reaffirming the ECB's decision and outlining the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's concerns about inflation due to the weak euro. \par \par \uc2  "Really, (the ECB sale) is not going to have a significant impact as far as flows go," said Andrew Delano, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at IDEAglobal. "But it's certainly a psychological boost to the euro, and it tells the market that the ECB is out there." \par \par \uc2  The ECB, which one analyst said seemed loath to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly "for fear it would be seen as panicking," emphasised in its announcement earlier that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sale would not count as a form of open \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders continued to assert that the floundering euro's only chance of being securely underpinned lay in joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  involving the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Chances of U.S. involvement, however, remained low, Delano said. \par \par \uc2  "The chances that the Treasury would all of a sudden turn around and change their perceived point of view and really consent to action that doesn't jibe with their long-held policy stance of a strong dollar are quite unlikely," Delano said. \par \par \uc2  December Swiss franc futures spiked higher after the ECB's announcement overnight, but then dropped below unchanged after the Swiss National Bank said it left its target three-month Swiss franc London Interbank Offered Rate (LIBOR) unchanged near the middle of the band between 3.0 and 4.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  The December yen contract traded sideways within a relatively narrow session range between $0.009474 and $0.009493 after the Bank of Japan's Policy Board announced it would leave its interest rates unchanged, as expected. \par \par \uc2  The recent focus on euro futures left many yen futures investors sidelined, traders said, as the market awaited further information regarding the size of Japan's supplementary budget expected to be set this fall. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures contracts dipped slightly upon release of a set of mild U.S. economic data that showed a dip in energy prices in August helped pull wholesale prices slightly lower. \par \par \uc2  December euros have resistance near last Thursday's one-week-high settlement at $0.87790, one analyst said. \par \par \uc2  Support for December yen was pegged at Wednesday's three-week low of $0.94650. \par \par \uc2  At 9:38 a.m. CDT (1438 GMT), December yen were $0.000005 lower at $0.009480, euros were off $0.00010 at $0.86530, sterling dropped $0.0004 to $1.4108, Swiss francs were off $0.0023 at $0.5683, Canadian dollars dropped $0.0028 to $0.6730, Australian dollars were $0.0057 lower at $0.5502, and Mexican pesos were off $0.000125 at $0.104300. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9e07x2a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
