\par\par \b \uc2  POLL-ECB unlikely to try euro rescue alone or with Fed. \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Stamp \par \uc2 840 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2000\par 15:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 7 (Reuters) - The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to attempt a rescue of the euro, simply because a solo market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would probably fail and the U.S. Federal Reserve has no interest in joining a bailout, economists say. \par \par \uc2  They put the chances of the central banks buying euros for dollars on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at little more than 20 percent, according to a Reuters poll. Talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  always surfaces when the euro plumbs new depths but as the euro wallowed around $0.8675 economists asked two rhetorical questions: \par \par \uc2  - why should the ECB risk its reputation with a solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  which is probably doomed to failure when the euro's plunge is doing little harm to the euro zone economy? \par \par \uc2  - why should the Fed help Europe when it believes Europe is to blame for the euro's troubles? \par \par \uc2  U.S. and European officials have plenty of opportunities to discuss a rescue for the euro, which hit record lows against the dollar on Thursday. This week European Union finance ministers and ECB Vice-President Christian Noyer will meet in France. Then later in the month finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors of the Group of Seven industrial nations meet in Prague before the annual meeting of the World Bank and International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  MAIDEN \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  But in the poll, taken on Thursday morning, economists saw little chance of any action. They gave only a 20 percent likelihood of the ECB making its maiden \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone by the end of this month, according to the median of 20 forecasts. \par \par \uc2  Also they saw only a marginally higher possibility of the U.S. and European central banks acting together to boost the euro, putting the likelihood at 23 percent. \par \par \uc2  Any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to stem a tide of capital out of the euro zone and into the United States not just by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators but more significantly by European investors buying U.S. companies to exploit the American economic boom. \par \par \uc2  "The ECB will realise that it has very little chance of success anyway. It would have to do it with the Fed," said Ian Gunner at ABN Amro in London. U.S. officials had long believed that Europe had not done enough to reform its economy to match the productivity gains which have made the U.S. economy so strong in the last three or four years. \par \par \uc2  "(Why should) the U.S. help to bail the euro out when the euro zone governments are not really doing anything to address the underlying symptoms of the euro's problems?" he said. \par \par \uc2  Germany, France and Italy have moved on tax and pension reform but what mattered was labour reform to make European workers more productive. Here Europe had done little to match the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Gunner. \par \par \uc2  ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone would have only a temporary effect. "If the ECB had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  this morning it would probably be up close to $0.90 by now," he said. "Then the market would wait to see what the Fed did this afternoon. If the Fed didn't do anything, it would probably come all the way back down again." The French government seems keen on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but other euro zone leaders are less enthusiastic. Indeed, the latest euro plunge began when markets got the impression German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder wasn't too worried about the weakness. \par \par \uc2  From an economic standpoint he may be right. "It is more of an image and political problem than a true economic problem," said Rui Martens dos Santos at Banco Portugues de Investimento in Lisbon. "The main target for the ECB is still the inflation rate and not the external image of the euro." \par \par \uc2  Euro zone inflation is a problem: it hit 2.4 percent in July, well above the ECB's 2.0 percent medium-term ceiling. But he said the weak euro was adding only a little to inflation through higher exports, compared with the impact of soaring oil prices. \par \par \uc2  WEAK SUPPORT \par \par \uc2  In technical terms he saw only weak support for the euro at $0.86. "The technical analysis is not favourable for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and market sentiment is not there to help. I think they would be wasting their time," he said. \par \par \uc2  Mike Moran at Standard Chartered was the only respondent to put the odds of a solo ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over 50 percent at 50-60. "If it does slip any more (the EU finance ministers) can't just sit aside and look helpless...I think they will try (but) I don't believe it will be particularly effective." \par \par \uc2  Herve Goulletquer at Credit Lyonnais in Paris ruled out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the Fed but raised the possibility of the Bank of Japan joining the ECB. The euro is also hitting record lows against the yen and Japanese officials are worried that a strong yen will hamper the country's weak economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  FOR DETAILS OF POLL PLEASE DOUBLE CLICK ON \par \par \uc2  (additional reporting by Joanne Russell). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9708tsq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
