\par\par \b \uc2  BARRON'S: Current Yield \b0\par\par\uc2 1,357 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 2000\par 07:42\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   It Won't Be A Day At The Beach For Greenspan & Co.\par   By William Pesek Jr.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The summer of 1999 was the hottest on record for Washington, D.C., but\par for Alan Greenspan the coming one may be even more uncomfortable. In\par fact, the Federal Reserve chairman and his colleagues may consider the\par months ahead unbearable.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  The heat is on at Fed headquarters. Policy makers raised short-term rates six times over the past 11 months and signs that growth is slowing remain tentative at best. Worse, for every indication that activity is cooling, there's another hinting that inflation may be heating up. Clearly, evidence of upward price pressures is equally tenuous, but they're likely to get more attention from Fed officials than any whiff of slowdown. \par \par \uc2  "Even if the economy slows, inflation trends will have a lot of say in what we do," a senior Fed official tells Barron's. \par \par \uc2  That sentiment continues to weigh on the fixed-income market. Increased volatility in equity markets drove some investors to the relative safety of bonds last week, driving yields lower. But the drop in rates may be short-lived. For one thing, the Fed isn't done tightening. And investors are growing worried that if the economy isn't already in the process of slowing, higher inflation may be inevitable. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, yields fell lower last week. The rate on the 10-year note ended the week at 6.33%, down from 6.51% a week earlier. The T-bond yielded 6.06% versus 6.22% a week earlier. Rates at the short end of the maturity spectrum also fell; the two-year note yield fell to 6.68% from 6.83% a week earlier. \par \par \uc2  The bond market's performance may suggest investors are less concerned about Fed rate hikes or inflation. If so, bond buyers may be disappointed. Fed Vice Chairman Roger Ferguson warned that low unemployment and strong consumer spending have made inflation a "very real" threat to the U.S. expansion. Such comments prompted UBS Warburg's chief economist, Joseph Carson, to ratchet up his Fed call for the year. \par \par \uc2  Following the Fed's recent half-percentage-point move, the largest in five years, Carson thinks it'll take another 100 basis points -- a full percentage point -- to keep inflation from accelerating and soothe bond investors. That would put the federal-funds rate at 7.50%. Carson's rationale is that demand and supply are getting too far out of whack, gross domestic product is too strong and a cyclical peak in market liquidity hasn't yet been reached. Most analysts think the Fed will go another 50-75 basis points. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Open Market Committee is still expected to tighten at its June 27-28 meeting, but a growing number of observers fear the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may go too far. Economists were struck by an unexpected 6.2% drop in existing-home sales in April, the biggest drop since January. But what's doing more to convince some analysts that the Fed's tightening cycle may be winding down are financial conditions. \par \par \uc2  Anthony Crescenzi, head of BondTalk.com, thinks those conditions may be tight enough to precipitate an economic slowdown. He points to a plunge in initial public offerings and April's anemic level of corporate bond issuance -- the lowest since September 1998. Meanwhile, spreads have widened considerably for corporate, agency and mortgage-backed debt, making the capital markets a less hospitable place for borrowers. And the Fed's quarterly survey of loan officers showed that banks have become "significantly more cautious." \par \par \uc2  All of this won't be good news for large companies that need to raise capital in the months ahead. One example is UAL, parent of United Air Lines, which last week announced plans to buy US Airways. A look at UAL's balance sheet suggests, aside from assuming $7.3 billion of US Air debt and leases, the airline will have to issue new debt to finance the acquisition. That's why investors weren't exactly cheering UAL's purchase; aside from possibly jeopardizing its credit quality, the carrier will pay dearly to tap the capital markets. \par \par \uc2  Of course, some may find opportunity in higher rates. Those looking for a silver lining in the 175 basis points worth of Fed tightening so far might take a look at corporate bond yields, notes Briefing.com strategist Tim Rogers. Corporate issuance has dried up recently as triple-A corporate financing rates rose above 8% -- the highest in five years. At the same time, the near-disappearance of new Treasury-bond issuance leaves the market pricing government bonds close to a 6% yield. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the spread between high-quality corporate debt and Treasury bonds last week was a whopping 190 basis points -- the biggest gap since the Treasury began selling 30-year debt in 1977. The divergence between Treasury and corporate debt may convince Fed officials that their tightenings are getting some traction. \par \par \uc2  The same may be true of volatility in asset markets. Roughly $3 trillion of equity-related wealth has been destroyed since March. Meanwhile, rates on 30-year mortgages are at a five-year high and on one-year adjustable-rate mortgages at a nine-year high. Clearly these changes in financial conditions have yet to slam the economy, but they may in the weeks ahead. The May employment report, due out Friday, may shed light on how higher rates are working their way through the economy. \par \par \uc2  Something else that may give the Fed pause is increasing volatility in emerging markets -- the last thing policy makers want, notes Jeremy Fand of Fleet Capital Markets. In Asia last week, the Philippine peso hit a 19-month low despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to tighten credit along with the Fed. The Indonesian rupiah and Korean won also have taken a hit since the Fed's rate hike. Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , too, have suffered their share of instability over the past couple of weeks. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the Fed is faced with a delicate balancing act. Policy makers don't want to send shock waves through the global economy. And at home, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to dampen growth without choking off investments that encourage both innovation and productivity. Crescenzi thinks that point may be at hand. Thanks to the Fed's actions to date, new and existing companies will enjoy less access to funds to allocate for capital projects. \par \par \uc2  It's a point underscored by the 6.4% plunge in durable goods in April, the worst drop in nine years. The challenge for the Fed is to avoid hampering innovation and the kinds of capital investments that precipitated the productivity boom that began in 1995. If the Fed continues to tighten the monetary noose, productivity could slip. That could mean higher inflation. \par \par \uc2  --- EUROPE'S SINGLE \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  RALLIED against the dollar last week, surprising investors around the globe. Indeed, many analysts doubt it will maintain the gains. Paradoxically, the euro's newfound strength came after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision not to raise rates in response to the Fed's recent move. It was seen as a hopeful sign the ECB is focusing on longer-term issues, not short-term gyrations in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It ended the week at 93.35 cents, versus 89.90 cents a week earlier. \par \par \uc2  Speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might soon \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets also boosted the euro. Feeding \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  hopes were comments by ECB officials and a move by European central banks to sell huge blocks of U.S. Treasuries. The sales had markets wondering if central banks were amassing cash for a large-scale euro-buying binge. ECB officials declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  But the ECB may not be willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  just yet. Officials fear that a failed attempt to boost the euro would damage its credibility. Besides, without cooperation from other Group of Seven members, especially the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not work. And so investors were left pleasantly surprised by the euro's performance last week but doubtful it will continue in the weeks ahead. \par \par \uc2  4079 5034 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010804dw5r00cvj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
