\par\par \b \uc2  Sterling subdued, eyes four-year lows vs dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 555 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2000\par 09:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 17 (Reuters) - Sterling remained soft on Wednesday, mired near the four-year lows against the dollar it had set a day earlier after the U.S. Federal Reserve raised U.S. interest rates above British ones. \par \par \uc2  While the Bank of England may not be finished with its own monetary tightening cycle yet, expectations that the Fed would be raising rates more aggressively in the coming months had undermined the pound, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The Fed raised its target for the federal funds rate by a half percentage point to 6.5 percent on Tuesday, as expected, and warned that the risks were biased mainly towards conditions that risked generating more inflationary pressure. \par \par \uc2  The BOE's key repo rate, on the other hand, has been kept steady at 6.0 percent for the past three months. \par \par \uc2  "After the rate move yesterday, people are going to start saying that the Fed is going to be more aggressive and that we are going to get more rate increases in the months ahead," said Audrey Childe-Freeman, European economist at CIBC World Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  "We can still expect another rate increase from the Bank of England probably in June so the perception of the rate differential between the U.S. and the UK is going to remain in favour of the dollar." Sterling was drifting within a half cent of a $1.4915 four-year low hit on Tuesday, which was more than 10 percent below its October 1999 peaks above $1.67. \par \par \uc2  The market's focus now was on Britain's average earnings figures, which could be key to a BOE rate decision at its next Monetary Policy Committee meeting in June and which were due to be released at 0830 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters have on average forecast a rise of 5.8 percent in average earnings in the three months to March. \par \par \uc2  "When you think that the Bank of England's reference value is 4.5 percent - that's the value consistent with the inflation target - we're probably still above where the Bank of England would be comfortable with," said Childe-Freeman. \par \par \uc2  The minutes of the BOE's latest MPC meeting were also due out at 0830 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a strong enough reading in the labour market data would underpin the pound, particularly against the euro, which was gradually ceding ground across the board after last week's rally. \par \par \uc2  Euro/sterling swooped down toward 60 pence, more than half a penny below Tuesday's peak of 60.84 pence, as the euro slipped below the psychological $0.90 level against the greenback. \par \par \uc2  "The euro looks like it's run out of steam," said a dealer at a Japanese bank in London, predicting a retest of record lows below 57 pence and $0.89 set earlier this month. \par \par \uc2  The euro's rally last week had been driven by wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  However, traders said the support offered by such wariness was dissipating due to an absence of such an official action and recent comments by German central bankers opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  without cooperation from the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  ((Shin Kishima, London Capital Markets +44-20-7542-6721, shinichi.kishima@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw5h00bjn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
