\par\par \b \uc2  Europe seen right not to bank on U.S. help on euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 775 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2000\par 14:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 16 (Reuters) - Europe is not banking on U.S. aid to weaken the dollar against the euro as such a move would raise the chances of electorally-damaging U.S. interest rate rises before November polls, analysts said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Hours before the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was widely expected to hike rates by half a point, Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke became the latest German central banker to say U.S. co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be vital in any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro. \par \par \uc2  But he went further and added that such assistance was unlikely before U.S. presidential elections in November. \par \par \uc2  U.S. exporters might welcome a decline in the value of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the euro given the dollar has appreciated as much as 32 percent against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since it was launched in January 1999, eroding their competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  But the U.S. authorities have stuck steadfastly to their policy of saying a strong dollar was in U.S. interests and are expected to focus on the inflation-curbing properties of dollar strength at a time when U.S. rates are already heading higher. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. Treasury won't want to be weakening the dollar and giving the Fed more reason to tighten during the presidential campaign," Paul Lambert, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  INFLATION-FIGHTING NEEDS HELP \par \par \uc2  U.S. monetary tightening is expected on Tuesday, with speculation rife that the Fed could hike rates by as much as a half percentage point - the first time in five years it would have moved rates by more than a quarter point in one go. \par \par \uc2  Expectations on how far the Fed would raise rates in the coming months have been revised higher in recent weeks and the U.S. would likely want all the help that dollar strength could offer in fighting inflation, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "If the Fed does 50 basis points it would send a really strong signal about how aggressive they are going to be," said Colin Clarkson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Sumitomo Bank. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the U.S. may also be reluctant to embark on any action which seems to signal a move away from the policy which has seen U.S. Treasury repeat ad nauseam in the past five years that a strong dollar was in the country's interests. \par \par \uc2  The last time the Federal Reserve was spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in concert with the Bundesbank was in 1995 when they were buying dollars, backing up the strong dollar policy the U.S. Treasury had adopted not long before. \par \par \uc2  Speculation there had been shift in this policy could surface if the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent its own money \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the dollar against the euro and risked making dollar-denominated assets less attractive to overseas investors, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  This would depress the capital inflows which are helping to finance the burgeoning U.S. current account deficit. Meanwhile, any sharp downturn in U.S. stocks would risk sending U.S. consumers to the poll feeling less wealthy. \par \par \uc2  "Why would the U.S. want to change the strong dollar policy which has served them so well?" said David Bloom, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  "What Welteke is saying is true and makes perfect sense, but it is just not what the market wants to hear right now, even though they always talk about wanting transparency." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. administration was expected to be all the more reluctant to tinker with the dollar's exchange rate against the euro ahead of the elections given trade imbalances with the euro zone were dwarfed by those with Asia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. trade deficit hit a record high of $29.2 billion in February but the deficit with Western Europe was almost half its deficit with Japan and two-thirds of its deficit with China. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar may be too strong against the euro but the trade imbalance is not with Europe, it is with Japan and Asia," said Ian Gunner, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  "Welteke's comments seem to show a fair amount of resignation to the fact that there won't be any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, given that a week can be a long time in the foreign exchanges, few were willing to take a punt on the chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the U.S. elections. \par \par \uc2  "It is too far away to think about and a lot could happen before then," said Gunner at ABN AMRO. \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets, +44 20 7542 6284, swaha.pattanaik@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw5g00h8y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
