\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro relieved as bears suffer  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  angst. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 966 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2000\par 08:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 10 (Reuters) - The euro boasted a rare bout of resilience on Wednesday as bears were forced to seriously consider the danger of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its defence. \par \par \uc2  Traders were also recycling talk of euro demand linked to a Japanese corporate acquisition in Europe, while further signs of a cooling in foreigners' long love affair with Japanese stocks helped keep the dollar firm on the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The market's suddenly not so sure the ECB (European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and there are even whispers of the U.S. joining in," said a U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "We can't see why the U.S. would have any interest in pushing the dollar lower, but we're also short (of the euro) so you have to be nervous," he added. \par \par \uc2  Late in the day the euro was up near session highs at $0.9080, having climbed from $0.8960 during New York trading hours. It also extended a bounce on the yen to 99.30 yen after rising offshore from around 97.80. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was holding firm at 109.37 yen , although a wave of offers from Japanese exporters had dragged it down from a two-month high overnight at 109.65. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was on everyone's lips after French Finance Minister Laurent Fabius made aggressive use of the word overnight to support his conviction that the euro would soon find a level more in line with fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  The frisson of fear was amplified by rumours in New York that the Financial Times newspaper would carry a report claiming the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready, if asked, to join concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the actual article, entitled "Is Europe misinterpreting signals from Washington", was not nearly as strong as rumour had it. \par \par \uc2  The report claimed no inside knowledge of the U.S. position, quoted no sources and merely noted that the United States had interevened to correct sharp movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  before, with the most recent cases in 1995 and 1998. \par \par \uc2  Still, dealers conceded the market was afraid something might be afoot among the Group of Seven (G7) industrialised nations and were reluctant to short the euro aggressively, if only for a while. \par \par \uc2  That opened the possibility of a bounce to resistance around $0.9170/80 in the near term, although they argued it could rally all the way to 96 cents and not threaten the long-term downtrend. \par \par \uc2  The euro's cause was aided by demand against the yen, said to be related to NTT Docomo's purchase of a 15 percent stake in Dutch firm KPN Mobile for five billion euros. \par \par \uc2  Its parent company, Nippon Telegraph and Telephone Corp , has been busy this week paying $5.5 billion for a U.S. Internet company and there was speculation it would join KPN in a joint bid for U.K. mobile group Orange Plc . \par \par \uc2  "It's got people wondering if this is the start of a trend: A new wave of Japanese investment offshore, which would go some way to recycling Japan's current account surplus and lessening the upward pressure on the yen," said the U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  It also coincided with further evidence of a foreign retreat from Japanese equities. Figures from the Tokyo Stock Exchange out on Tuesday showed foreigners sold a net 846 billion yen of stocks, the largest monthly amount in 10 years. \par \par \uc2  There was also suspicion Japanese investors were being tempted by the high yields on offer in the United States. \par \par \uc2  The spread between U.S. and Japanese 10-year yields has widened sharply in recent weeks to a juicy 492 basis points as the market priced in a more aggressive Federal Reserve tightening. \par \par \uc2  San Francisco Federal Reserve President Robert Parry did nothing to challenge the market view, saying on Tuesday that even if the Fed veers from its gradualist monetary policy, it still would be consistent with a cautious approach to slowing growth. \par \par \uc2  The comment cemented the market's belief the Fed would raise rates by 50 basis points at its meeting next week, a step up in pace from its past five quarter-point moves. \par \par \uc2  Dealers cautioned, however, that higher rates carried risks for the dollar as well. \par \par \uc2  At the moment the market was assuming the U.S. economy would enjoy a soft landing, but a more aggressive Fed could stir fears of a sharper slowdown, which would add to pressure on stocks and maybe spook offshore investors out of U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  "After all, you could argue that rates are rising in part because they're having to pay more to attract the foreign money they need to fund the trade deficit," said the U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "That's a sign of weakness, not strength." ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0557 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab      Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   1999 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab   09 May.\par \tab \tab \tab      -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr\tab \tab   0.9077   0.9073   +0.04    -9.86   1.0070\par  Dlr/yen\tab \tab    109.36   109.20   +0.15    +7.00   102.21\par  Eur