\par\par \b \uc2  Financial heavyweights backing euro  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Apu Sikri \par \uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Mai 2000\par 20:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 5 (Reuters) - A growing chorus of influential financial figures is calling for official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster the euro, saying markets have been too tough on Europe's fledgling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It's a clear case of overshoot, and it will probably continue until the authorities take a stand," U.S. financier George Soros said this week as the euro plunged to new record lows against the dollar and yen. \par \par \uc2  "The authorities have to show their hand. They have to stand up and be seen. I think the market will take their lead," Soros said in London on Thursday. His past large \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bets have made him a force in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  world. \par \par \uc2  The euro has lost nearly 25 percent of its value against the dollar and the yen since its launch by 11 European nations in January 1999. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which started its life trading at $1.1747 16 months ago, was hovering on Friday at about a cent above its $0.8840 record low reached on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Japan's former vice finance minister, Eisuke Sakakibara, on Friday added his voice to the chorus of those urging \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying policymakers "need to take some market action. They talk a lot but no action has been taken, so now the market knows." \par \par \uc2  Known during his official tenure for his advocacy of official action on behalf of the Japanese yen, Sakakibara said that "if I was in that position, I would not let the market overshoot." \par \par \uc2  Those views appeared to find resonance even in some parts of official Europe. French Prime Minister Lionel Jospin called on Wednesday for a "collective response" from the world's major governments to the euro's decline. \par \par \uc2  Many market watchers say the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline is out of line with Europe's improving economies, a point emphasised by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  vice president Christian Noyer, who called the euro's current exchange rate "ridiculous". \par \par \uc2  Market "overshoot" occurs when financial markets push the value of a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher or lower than justified by economic fundamentals of inflation, interest rates and growth in relation to other economies. \par \par \uc2  "The extreme volatility of the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been a problem. Something should be done about it," Paul Volcker, former chairman of the U.S. Federal Reserve Board, said at a panel discussion on the euro in New York last month. \par \par \uc2  But despite the appeals for action on the euro's behalf, opinion remains divided on whether governmental \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can effectively reverse market momentum. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  has generally eschewed the idea, arguing that markets self-correct over time and that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if unsuccessful, can do more harm than good. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers, asked about the euro's weakness, reiterated on Wednesday that "our approach has always been to concentrate on economic fundamentals." \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the euro's weakness in part reflects the slow European pace of implementing structural reforms that would help lower chronic unemployment, increase productivity and boost economic growth. \par \par \uc2  While many European officials argue the euro is too weak, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) has abstained from any market action and officials have given mixed signals about the possibility of future \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "There are two views on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . One is that the ECB needs to do something to change sentiment, that this is an overshoot and coordinated multinational \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past has worked," said Mohamad El-Erian, a money manager at the Pacific Investment Management Co. \par \par \uc2  "Against that you have the concern that this may be viewed as a sign of weakness rather than strength," he said. \par \par \uc2  A weak euro has its advantages. It has boosted exports by making European goods cheaper to buyers from other countries with stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But that advantage was being overshadowed by the risk of importing inflation into the continent. The inflation threat comes from purchases of imports whose prices go up as the euro falls. \par \par \uc2  "The euro has gotten to a stage where competitiveness issues are being overwhelmed by potential negative inflationary influences," El-Erian said. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw55015qa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
