\par\par \b \uc2  Euro's woes seen greater if ECB springs rate shock. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 13:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 1 (Reuters) - The euro remains at risk whatever the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does with its interest rates this week but its losses will be larger if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  springs a surprise rate rise, analysts said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Expectations the ECB will leave rates on hold at Thursday's meeting have grown after recent comments by central bankers. This has contributed to a slide which culminated on Monday with the euro suffering its biggest one-day drop since birth. \par \par \uc2  While an increase in rates would raise the return investors get for holding euros, it could end up triggering a fresh run on the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by fanning concern the ECB is taking its eye off its main mandate - a strict inflation target and not the exchange rate per se. \par \par \uc2  "At this point raising rates would be worse than leaving them unchanged," said Keith Lewis, manager treasury markets at American Express Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "If they do nothing there might be a short-term negative reaction in the euro but if they raise rates it would be seen as a desperate measure to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than being done for the right reasons." \par \par \uc2  ECB officials have pointed out time and again that the euro's fall has a limited impact on inflation as external trade accounts for a smaller proportion of the area's national income than it did for individual members. \par \par \uc2  However, inflation has not been immune to the 14 percent decline seen in as many months in broad measures of the euro's value, particularly against the backdrop of rising oil prices. \par \par \uc2  Data on Tuesday showed the euro zone's inflation rate hit two-year highs and the ECB's 2.0 percent ceiling in January. \par \par \uc2  This, together with the robustness of manufacturing survey reports from the 11-nation euro zone, means a case can be made for raising rates sooner rather than later, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  However, the ECB could find such arguments falling on deaf ears, given traders' current mindset. \par \par \uc2  "It is difficult to see any substantial upside for the euro given the campaign of vilification against the ECB is continuing unabated," said Mark Cliffe, chief economist at ING Barings. \par \par \uc2  "If they do nothing then people will say the ECB is falling behind the curve and they are doing nothing to help the euro but if they do raise rates, people will say they are panicking." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  LURKS IN ECB'S ARMOURY \par \par \uc2  The other option open to the ECB is to raise rates and then follow up with aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on foreign exchanges, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The extent of the euro's gyrations this week mean traders are already on edge about the possibility of such action. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  jitters swept through the market on Tuesday, lifting the euro more than a cent within minutes to a high of $0.9864. That rebound came only a day after the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost 3.5 percent in six hours to plumb life lows near $0.94. \par \par \uc2  The gyrations translate into a 10 pfenning swing in old dollar/mark terms and mean that any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be presented as an attempt to smooth excessive market volatility. \par \par \uc2  "The potential for zany moves which are not based on any fundamentals is high at the moment, as has already been amply demonstrated this week," said Cameron Crise, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Warburg Dillon Read in London. \par \par \uc2  However, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen as a dangerous strategy for the ECB to embark on given that its first such public foray into the foreign exchanges on its own account would colour traders' perception of its ability to get its own way in the future. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said success would depend on how much money the ECB was prepared to throw at the foreign exchanges and whether it could secure the backing of other industrial powers - crucially the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could work but it is a high risk strategy," said Cliffe at ING. \par \par \uc2  "They need to burn a lot of fingers and the question is whether the market is short enough so that they can. There is a less than 10 percent chance of this happening." \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 6284, swaha.pattanaik@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw3105u6c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
