\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar surges to 5-month highs vs yen. \b0\par\par\uc2      By Lida Poletz \par \uc2 847 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2000\par 23:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      NEW YORK, Feb 8 (Reuters) - The dollar, buoyed by a \par \par \uc2  government report showing labour costs fell despite a tight job \par \par \uc2  market, powered to five-month highs against the yen on Tuesday \par \par \uc2  and countered a rally attempt by the euro. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  spurted higher after the U.S. Labour \par \par \uc2  Department reported nonfarm productivity jumped a \par \par \uc2  stronger-than-expected 5.0 percent in the fourth quarter, and \par \par \uc2  unit labour costs surprised with a 1.0 percent drop. \par \par \uc2      The data also triggered a rally in inflation-wary U.S. \par \par \uc2  stock and bond markets, helping to prop up the greenback. \par \par \uc2      "In the grand scheme of things this is a second-tier report \par \par \uc2  but it is a very dollar-friendly number," said Jim McCormick, \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan. \par \par \uc2      Analysts polled by Reuters had been expecting productivity \par \par \uc2  to rise by 4.2 percent and the cost of labour to climb by 0.8 \par \par \uc2  percent. \par \par \uc2      The dollar, which had hit its highest level since September \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par against the yen\tab   overnight before succumbing to selling\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par pressure, built up a second head of steam after the data and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par pressed to a fresh high of 109.69 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But it met stiff resistance around the psychologically\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par significant 110-yen level, traders said and closed just under\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 109.50, a gain of 0.75 percent on the day.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "At every layer from this point on, the market expects it\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to be very tough to break the upside," said Jeffrey Yu, senior\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dealer at Sanwa Bank in New York. "Despite all this, there are\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par buyers waiting in the wings to buy dollar/yen."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yen has been under pressure since Japanese officials\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par said at the weekend that gross domestic product (GDP) probably\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par contracted for the second quarter in a row - a technical\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par recession - in the final quarter of 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Against the euro, the dollar ended softer compared\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to its Monday New York close after ceding ground during\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par European trade. But the productivity data helped the U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  claw back much of the territory it had lost.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro had climbed more than a cent against the dollar as\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Germany's blue-chip DAX stock index rallied more than 3 percent\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and German unemployment fell for the fourth straight month.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Data released by Germany's Federal Labour Office showed\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par unemployment fell to 3.959 million in January, adjusted for\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par seasonal factors, giving a jobless rate of 10.1 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But rather than extend gains in U.S. trade, the euro\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par instead was knocked back from an intraday high of 99.13 cents\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to around 98.50 at the U.S. close, compared to its 98.08-cent\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par New York Monday finish.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The market failed to respond to comments from European\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief economist Otmar Issing, who repeated he\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par believed the euro would rise as growth in the 11-nation euro\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par zone started to outpace that of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 United States\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Asked whether the ECB could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par markets to defend the euro, Issing said: "It's a question of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the right moment, the right occasion, the right timing, the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par right situation and the conviction that it will work."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But he added that those conditions had not been fulfilled.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Many analysts said the U.S. productivity report justified\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the Federal Reserve's cautious approach to hiking interest\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rates to engineer a soft landing for a booming economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Until you start to see signs of imbalances in the U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par that have an impact on the inflation and Fed story, it is going\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to be a slow and gradual recovery in the euro," McCormick\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Fed has hiked rates four times in the past six months\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and has hinted it may tighten more. But the Fed's moves have\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par been gradual, by a quarter percentage point each time.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((U.S. Financial Markets Desk, 212 859 1868))\par 