\par\par \b \uc2  Ex-Fed's Blinder says no U.S. rate hike needed. \b0\par\par\uc2 591 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 1999\par 02:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SAN FRANCISCO, Sept. 27 (Reuters) - Former Federal Reserve vice-chairman Alan Blinder said Monday he does not see justification for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates at its policy-setting meeting next week. \par \par \uc2  "If I were still a member of the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC), I wouldn't see a reason to raise interest rates" on Oct. 5, Blinder told reporters after addressing a National Association for Business Economics conference in San Francisco. \par \par \uc2  The FOMC raised official U.S. rates at back-to-back meetings in June and August and convenes again next week to debate monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The former No. 2 at the Fed said he and other economists are "basically clueless" when it comes to explaining how U.S. unemployment has lingered around 4-1/4 percent over the past year without generating inflation. \par \par \uc2  But given the fading credibility of Fed econometric models which not so long ago predicted, for instance, that inflation would rise if unemployment dropped below 6.0 percent, Blinder said it is becoming increasingly difficult for the Fed to stage preemptive strikes on inflation. \par \par \uc2  "I gave a few speeches when I was on the (Fed) board about the importance of being preemptive," Blinder said. "And those arguments were correct. But they were predicated on having a model of the economy that you 'believed in'. You knew it was imperfect, it could make mistakes. But the model gave you guidance that was better than throwing darts at a newspaper." \par \par \uc2  When the Fed loses confidence in its models, and Blinder said it is likely that they have, it makes sense to be less preemptive. \par \par \uc2  "It's sensible for the Fed to do that under the circumstances and that's what they have been doing," he said. \par \par \uc2  Answering questions about the dollar's depreciation against the Japanese yen lately and its implications for Japan's economy, Blinder said he saw it as a serious threat to the fragile recovery in Asia. \par \par \uc2  "I am inclined to think that the exchange rate is quite important and inclined to think that a rising yen is a serious peril to the Japanese economy. I think it's terribly important to, in the first instance, stop the yen from rising and in the second instance, actually turn it around and have it fall." \par \par \uc2  Japan had made enormous strides by pushing fiscal and monetary stimulus about to the limit and must now try to maintain the stimulus, Blinder said. But the potential for a drop in demand due to the higher yen poses the threat of renewed recession, he added. \par \par \uc2  Blinder said he thought it was unlikely the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on behalf of Japan to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  "This is not really a problem for the United States, the dollar/yen exchange rate. It may be eventually, but sure isn't now," he said. "I believe that in the long run the dollar is going to be much, much lower compared to the yen" because of the U.S. current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  "The question for the U.S. Treasury is to what extent it's willing to act not in the interests of the United States particularly but in the interest of other countries especially Japan," Blinder said. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, Blinder said he would applaud such U.S. support which he thinks would have broader benefits for the global economy by aiding Japan. \par \par \uc2  ((-N.A. Treasury desk, 212-859-1660)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9s033xe\par