\par\par \b \uc2  Effectiveness of US-Japan  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  questioned. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 1999\par 13:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 28 (Reuters) - Concerted joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  may halt the yen's uptrend, but many dealers doubt the effect would be long-lasting. \par \par \uc2  Determination by both countries to correct fundamental problems, such as the widening trade imbalance, is necessary to fully stem the yen's appreciation, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Growing prospects for economic recovery in Japan has encouraged yen buying since this summer. The yen rose as high as 103.20 to the dollar on September 15, up 15 percent since June. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not expected to occur, though, without Japan taking further monetary steps to counter the yen's appreciation. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will happen so easily...at least for some time," said Hank Note, senior vice president at Sumitomo Bank. \par \par \uc2  Note said joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may initially push up the dollar by about five yen, but he doubts it would lift the dollar sharply above 110 yen. \par \par \uc2  Speculation such joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may occur grew overnight, though, following comments by U.S. Federal Reserve and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  ECB Vice President Christian Noyer said on Monday that the ECB would be willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if it deemed it necessary to stablise the market. \par \par \uc2  Separately, New York Fed President William McDonough said: "It impresses me that the G7 statement was very sound and Japan has made a very clear statement in my view of what their intentions are. That is a very positive step." \par \par \uc2  He was referring to the statement issued after finance ministers and central banker chiefs from the Group of Seven industrialised nations met on Saturday in Washington. \par \par \uc2  But history proves that joint \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not necessarily change the direction of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  the way monetary authorities intend, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Between 1993 and 1995, Japan and other nations \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to stem the dollar's decline, yet the dollar, after time, continued to drop in value. \par \par \uc2  In 1993, Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times between April and August when the dollar fell from around 112 yen to 101 yen. \par \par \uc2  But the action was only temporarily successful in halting the dollar's decline, which began in early 1993 after U.S. officials said that they wanted to see a stronger yen to correct the widening trade imbalance between the two countries. \par \par \uc2  The joint dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued in 1994 and 1995 after the dollar dipped below 100 yen, with Germany and other central banks joining Japan and the U.S. Still, the dollar kept declining, tumbling to a post-war record low of 79.75 yen on April 19, 1995. \par \par \uc2  The trade imbalance is now again an issue between Japan and the United States, with the U.S. global trade deficit on track to shatter last year's record of 164 billion dollars. In the first seven months of 1999, the U.S. trade deficit totalled 144 billion dollars, up 59 percent over the same period in 1998. \par \par \uc2  Yet concern that U.S. share prices have risen too far, and are set to tumble, is another factor behind the dollar's decline in value. \par \par \uc2  Takashi Ueno, general manager of investment strategy division at Tokyo-Mitsubishi Securities, said: "The U.S. had claimed the yen's rise is the problem of Japan, but can they really say that?" \par \par \uc2  "Global financial markets are worried that the U.S. stock market is threatened by a steep decline," Ueno said. "The U.S. should consider taking more aggressive action to tackle the problem of the yen's appreciation as doing so may save its own markets as well as the global economy." ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432-8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9s030rp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
