\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Yen-weakening efforts seen hinging on BOJ. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 1999\par 11:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 27 (Reuters) - The yen's stranglehold on the dollar is unlikely to be broken until the Bank of Japan comes up with steps to convince markets that international concern about its strength will be backed by action, analysts and dealers say. \par \par \uc2  Despite an unusually blunt statement from the Group of Seven at the weekend expressing concern at the yen's strength, it slipped only briefly in Tokyo on Monday before rebounding to pre-G7 levels around 104 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Traders said operators were testing the resolve of the Japanese authorities and other G7 members by buying the yen until they see some form of official action - either yen-selling market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or some form of monetary easing by the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had jumped above 106 yen in early trade on the belief that Japan had agreed to take some extraordinary monetary or fiscal steps in return for a strong statement of concern about the yen in the communique. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say a BOJ easing is a pre-requisite for joint \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it is necessary to offset the Japanese government's aggressive fiscal policy, which risks pushing up interest rates and the yen. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ fuelled hopes that it would take easing steps with a rare statement of its own after the G7, saying its monetary policy would respond to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market developments. But traders were sceptical whether the independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would swallow its pride and succumb to external pressure. \par \par \uc2  "The yen gradually regained its strength as the consensus that the BOJ may not be able to come up with effective monetary measures increased significantly," said Masayuki Yamamoto, assistant vice president at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  That view was reinforced when the BOJ kept its stance in the money market unchanged by leaving the usual net surplus of about one trillion yen in a regular morning \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Although it was unlikely the BOJ would take immediate action after the G7 meeting, the market had still been hoping for some concrete measures by the BOJ," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  A senior Japanese trust bank dealer said comments by BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami in Washington, while hinting at possible steps to ease policy, contained nothing on concrete action to tackle the high-flying yen. \par \par \uc2  "Looking closely at Hayami's comments, they basically didn't differ from those made last week after the BOJ Policy Board meeting. That seems to have depressed yen sellers," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  With confusion reining over what, if anything, would be done in the wake of the G7 meeting, dealers said the market may test the dollar towards a recent low of 103.20 yen set on September 15, its lowest since December 1995. \par \par \uc2  If that level is broken, the market is likely to begin an assault on the key 100 yen level. \par \par \uc2  The yen stood at 104.28/33 per dollar in late afternoon trade on Monday, after jumping to 103.90 on active dollar sales by foreign operators and exporters. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was expected to find near-term resistance at 106 yen, a Tokyo high on Monday. Stronger resistance was seen around 106.50 and 107.50 yen, traders said. \par \par \uc2  In the near term, customarily strong dollar-buying interest against the yen ahead of interim corporate book closings at the end of September and uncertainty over the direction of Tokyo stocks could make dealers careful about expanding their yen positions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Many U.S. operators, including hedge funds and U.S. pension funds, have been spotted converting profits made on Japanese assets, primarily stocks, back into dollars, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Many dealers had second thoughts about bidding the yen beyond a recent low of 103.20 after watching the steep decline in the Nikkei average late last week," a European bank dealer said. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432-8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9r0326g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
