\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Yen center of attention as G7 ministers meet. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vittorio Alessio \par \uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 1999\par 22:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Sept 25 (Reuters) - Major industrial nations agree a strong yen is a problem for the fragile Japanese economy, but will leave it to Tokyo to pump more money into the system to prop up growth and lower the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value, a financial official said on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  "There is consensus on the fact that yen strength is a problem," the Italian Treasury official told reporters as Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers met on Saturday to discuss the yen and the dangers its recent strength poses for Japan's nascent economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The financial leaders were also to discuss aid to poor nations as the world's economic outlook begins to brighten. Russia's economic problems and allegations of widespread corruption and money laundering were also expected to top the agenda. \par \par \uc2  FINANCIAL MARKETS NERVOUSLY AWAIT OUTCOME \par \par \uc2  Financial market attention was riveted on the outcome of talks on the yen, which has risen about 14 percent against the dollar since early July. The gain has been large enough to spark fears it could make Japanese exports too expensive and throw Japan's recovery off track. \par \par \uc2  The officials - from Britain, Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan and the United States - signalled that the yen would be prominent on their agenda in advance of the talks, taking place on the sidelines of the World Bank and IMF annual meetings. \par \par \uc2  Japan would like the support of its G7 partners in its battle to tame the high flying yen. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been reticent about the idea of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and wanted Tokyo to go further to reflate its economy and boost domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  COMMON VOICE SOUGHT FROM JAPAN \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's G7 partners would like the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Bank of Japan to settle their differences over the issue of injecting more money into the economy. The Bank of Japan said last Tuesday that it was not going to inject more liquidity into the economy. \par \par \uc2  In a measure of the tension between Tokyo and Washington on the issue, the U.S. Treasury followed up a bilateral meeting with Japan with an unusually brief statement describing the talks as "a good, full discussion." \par \par \uc2  Later during a photo session shortly after the gathering of financial leaders got underway, Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa was asked by a reporter if his counterparts in the G7 talks had "beaten you up." \par \par \uc2  "I've survived," Miyazawa replied. \par \par \uc2  The G7 partners were also expected to urge the United States to do more to boost savings and curb its seemingly insatiable appetite for foreign goods. A string of record trade deficits has unnerved financial markets and helped fuel the rise in the yen. \par \par \uc2  They were also expected to express pleasure at improved economic growth in Europe but also stress a need to undertake more structural reforms in their economies. \par \par \uc2  RUSSIAN PROBLEMS ON AGENDA \par \par \uc2  Russia, which will take part in only a brief part of the half-day talks, is expected to face demands that it put its economy in order by stamping out corruption, improving tax collection and undertaking further economic reform if it wants to continue to receive western aid. \par \par \uc2  Although it does not feature on the official agenda, the talks will also touch on embarrassing allegations of money laundering involving the Bank of New York  and other U.S. banks. The scandal has raised questions about the fate of International Monetary Fund (IMF) loans to the Russia. \par \par \uc2  The group also was expected to pave the way for a deal to be agreed upon in the International Monetary Fund which will provide cash for aid to the world's poorest countries by dint of a complex scheme to revalue 14 million ounces of IMF gold. \par \par \uc2  Full approval for this deal is expected to emerge in international meetings over the next few days. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9p03112\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
