\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asia stocks dive after Wall Street sell-off. \b0\par\par\uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 1999\par 03:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SYDNEY, Sept 24 (Reuters) - Asian share markets started Friday on the back foot, pressured by a large fall on Wall Street and signs the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not back joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the yen's strength. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are expected to be reasonably quiet on Friday, adopting a cautious stance ahead of the meeting of G7 finance ministers in Washington over the weekend, with the U.S. dollar edging back above 104 yen in early Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo stocks opened sharply lower after the overnight losses in Nikkei December futures in Chicago and Wall Street, breaching the 17,000 level in early trade for the first time since June 15. \par \par \uc2  At 0100 GMT, the Nikkei was down 512.65 points, or 2.96 percent, at 16,813.11. At the same time, the Australian stock exchange was down over 1.2 percent at 2,891.8. \par \par \uc2  "It was a pretty ordinary night offshore, obviously...and after holding up for two days it is not surprising to see a bit of a sell-off here, this morning," Burrell & Co broker Richard Herring said in Sydney. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average fell 205.48 points to close at 10,318.59 on Thursday, its lowest finish since April 9. \par \par \uc2  The index also lost 225 points on Tuesday, making it only the second time in history it had recorded two losses of over 200 points in a week. \par \par \uc2  The Nasdaq Composite fell 108.33 points or 3.79 percent, the fourth largest point loss for the index to close at 2,749.83. Japanese stocks will not only be affected by the Wall Street losses but also, given the U.S. reluctance to sanction joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the strength of the yen. \par \par \uc2  There are concerns that while the yen strength reflects improving economic conditions in Japan, too rapid a rise could imperil the recovery and so is a negative for the Nikkei. \par \par \uc2  Market hopes that the U.S. would back joint \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this weekend's G7 meeting were dashed by U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers overnight, who said Japan should use whatever measures it can to boost its economy. \par \par \uc2  The USD/yen was at 104.48/58 yen at 0100 GMT, boosted by bids from Japanese investors, from New York's late 103.83/91. \par \par \uc2  If the meeting produces no signs of agreement to stem the yen's strength, then the yen is expected to rally strongly next week. \par \par \uc2  "I think fairly quiet ahead of the meeting, but obviously I think after the meeting the risk is that we do go lower and do move towards that 100 (yen) level next week," Deutsche Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist in Sydney, Tim Moloney said of the U.S. dolar prosepcts \par \par \uc2  "From what we heard from Summers last night, coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the moment is not appropriate," he said. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in the region, New Zealand markets were unpleasantly surprised on Friday by data showing the economy had contracted 0.3 percent in the June quarter, when a rise of 0.5 percent had been expected. \par \par \uc2  The news saw the NZ dollar lose almost a cent as it fell to a low of $0.5145 from a high of $0.5235, with the Kiwi's losses knocking the Australian dollar back to support at $0.6480. \par \par \uc2  ((Sydney newsroon 61-2-9373-1800, sydney.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9o035in\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscale