\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar plays waiting game vs yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 896 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 1999\par 13:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 23 (Reuters) - The dollar stuttered higher against the yen by the European midsession on Thursday, but was hampered by market caution ahead of a meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers in Washington on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  Expectations are fading that the G7 meeting will result in joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's rapid rise and so protect Japan's recovering economy, but trade remained hesitant ahead of the meeting, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Volume was further dampened by a market holiday on Thursday in Japan, they added. \par \par \uc2  "The market does not have a strong directional feel, but people are watching any statements from financial officials fairly closely," said Johnathan Bayley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Paribas in Paris. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 104.15/20 yen at 0954 GMT, from an earlier one-week low of 103.44 and from 104.03/12 in late European trade on Wednesday. The dollar reached a 3-1/2-year low of 103.17 yen on September 15. \par \par \uc2  Doubts the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  would come to Japan's rescue with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  intensified after overnight comments from Federal Reserve Bank of New York President William McDonough. \par \par \uc2  McDonough said current dollar/yen levels are not a concern for the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  "We do not foresee concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless the Nikkei index falls," said Fabio Stolfa, \b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Caboto Milan. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen could go to 90-95." \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to post-World War II lows of 80 yen in April 1995, before U.S.-Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  started it on an ascent which reached highs of 148 yen in August, 1998. \par \par \uc2  TOKYO STOCKS FALL \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the generally buoyant Nikkei 225 average closed down 3.39 percent or 607 ponts, its largest one-day point drop on a closing basis so far this year. \par \par \uc2  The euro edged up to 108.55/74 yen from an earlier low of 108.15, but was softer from 109.31/110.30 in Thursday's late European trade. \par \par \uc2  The euro also dipped against the dollar, to $1.0448/53 from $1.0501/04 in late European trade on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll of 38 analysts showed unanimous expectations the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep interest rates steady at 2.5 percent after its meeting on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Consumer prices fell in the German states of Hesse, Baden-Wuerttemberg and Bavaria in September from the previous month, by 0.2 percent, 0.2 percent and 0.4 percent respectively. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was soft compared with Wednesday's 4-1/2-month peaks against the dollar above $1.64, although the Confederation of British Industry's September industrial trends survey showed the manufacturing orders balance was the least negative since March 1998. \par \par \uc2  Bank of England Governor Eddie George said on Wednesday in Mexico the pound's strength after the September 8 quarter-point rate hike to 5.25 percent was "not welcomed". \par \par \uc2  The pound was trading at $1.6322/32 at 1037 GMT. The euro was at 63.98/05 pence, from a life low of 63.73 pence set on Tuesday. ((Carolyn Cohn, London Capital Markets +44 171 542 6320, carolyn.cohn@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  Doubts the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  would come to Japan's rescue with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  intensified after